WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:07.560
- Hello. Good afternoon, everybody.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:10.110
We're so happy to see you here.

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:14.580
I'm just going to say a few
words about the Park lecture.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.290
The Seong-Yawng Park

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:19.320
and Marguerite Clark Park Memorial Lecture

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:24.270
was made possible by a generous
gift from their estate.

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:26.430
The principle objective of this gift is

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:29.970
to foster the study of Korea at Yale

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:32.700
by bringing recognition
of the Korean Peninsula

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.980
and its affairs and achievements

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:38.283
both to Yale and to the wider community.

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:44.150
The Parks met at Yale
and were married in 1964.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:48.990
And in an interview Park gave in 2002,

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:51.570
he said that coming to the United States

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:54.750
and studying at Yale was an experience

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.930
that shaped his entire life.

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:02.520
Park earned his PhD at Yale in 1965,

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.800
and then taught economics

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:08.490
at the University of California Berkeley.

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:13.110
In 1968, he returned to Seoul
to work as an economic advisor

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:14.973
to the president of Korea,

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:20.413
and he also taught economics
at Sangmyung University.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:28.700
In 1984, he took over the helm
of Kumho after his father,

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:33.030
who was its founder and
chairman, passed away.

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:35.850
Park continued to build
this transportation,

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:39.210
construction, and electronics company,

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:43.080
establishing Asiana Airlines in 1988,

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:47.220
and thereby forming the Kumho group.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:51.390
The inaugural Park Memorial
lecture was by Victor Cha,

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:53.910
professor of Government
and International Affairs

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:55.533
at Georgetown University.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:00.630
Carter Eckhart, Yoon Se Young
Professor of Korean history

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:04.440
at Harvard University, gave
the second Park lecture,

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:08.010
and Jin Y. Park, professor
of philosophy and religion

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:11.490
at American University, gave the third.

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:13.950
And today, it is our great pleasure

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:16.623
to welcome Laurel Kendall,

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:20.160
who is our first anthropologist

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:21.780
to give the Park lecture at Yale.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:23.400
So, thank you so very much.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:31.470
- Okay. Thank you so much for coming.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:32.790
My name is Hwansoo Kim.

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:34.830
I teach Korean religion and culture

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:37.080
in the Department of Religious Studies.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:41.070
And it's great honor to
introduce our speaker,

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:43.080
Dr. Laurel Kendall.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:48.080
Dr. Kendall is the curator of
Asian Ethnographic Collections

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:52.620
at the American Museum of Natural History,

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:54.330
and a senior research scholar

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:57.630
at the Weatherhead East Asian Institute

00:02:57.630 --> 00:02:59.220
at Columbia University,

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:01.770
and also a former president

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:06.600
of the Association for Asian
Studies in 2016 to 2017.

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:11.670
She earned her BA from UC Berkeley,

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:16.593
as well as her two MA
and a PhD from Columbia.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:21.690
She is an uncontested,
unparalleled authority

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:24.690
on the subject in Korean shamanism

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:29.580
and her broader research
ranges from shamanism

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:32.400
and gender relations
and religious practice

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.273
and material religion in
East and Southeast Asia.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:42.000
Dr. Kendall began her
passion for Korean studies

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.350
when she was working as
a Peace Corps in 1970

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:47.493
when I was born.

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:49.740
That's long time ago.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:57.690
And she encountered several female shamans

00:03:57.690 --> 00:03:59.820
through their ritual performances,

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:04.260
and this encounter led
her to three decades

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:06.750
of anthropological fieldwork,

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:10.830
resulting in three
groundbreaking monographs.

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:15.180
First, "Shamans, Housewives,
and Other Restless Spirits:

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:20.040
Women in Korean Ritual
Life" published in 1985

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:22.470
examines Korean female shamans

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:25.233
with a special focus on gender relations.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:26.933
Her second book,

00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:29.520
"Life and Hard Times of a Korean Shaman,"

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:31.590
published in 1988,

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:35.880
details the life of a Korean
shaman, Yongsu's mother,

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:40.110
who became a close
informant and her friend,

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:42.453
but who passed away
last year unfortunately.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:46.920
Lastly, "Shamans, Nostalgias, and the IMF:

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:50.070
South Korean Popular Religion in Motion,"

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:54.360
published in 2009, traces the
evolution of Korean shamanism

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:59.360
from being disparaged as
superstition in the 1970s

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:02.310
to be embraced as a religion,

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:05.250
and ultimately being noted
as a fundamental part

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:09.090
of Korean culture in the 1990s.

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:12.000
And actually, I have been using this book

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.000
for like 10 years for my class,

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:17.850
religion and culture in Korea.

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:20.880
In addition to her expertise
in Korean shamanism,

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:25.327
Dr. Kendall has also published
a book on gender titled,

00:05:25.327 --> 00:05:29.250
"Getting Married in Korea" in 1996.

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:32.520
This book concerned how matches are made,

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:34.710
wedding rights are conducted,

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:38.790
and families finance and carry
out the recorded exchange

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:42.450
of ceremonial goods in South Korea.

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:45.570
The books shed light on
the changing material...

00:05:45.570 --> 00:05:46.403
Not material.

00:05:46.403 --> 00:05:49.590
Marital roles of Korean women in a society

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:54.000
rapidly transformed in
the late 20th century

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:56.160
by industrial capitalism

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:59.133
and the emergence of a
full-blown consumer culture.

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:03.720
Dr. Kendall's most recent
work concerns the production

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:06.450
and consumption of sacred objects

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:09.030
in contemporary market economies,

00:06:09.030 --> 00:06:12.420
and she has conducted extensive
field work in South Korea,

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:15.840
Vietnam, Myanmar, and Bali.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:18.547
And this research resulted in a book,

00:06:18.547 --> 00:06:20.610
"Mediums and Magical Things:

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:25.140
Statues, Paintings, and
Masks in Asian Places."

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:29.643
This book was published
two years ago in 2021.

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:34.740
This book describes how
material images give form

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:38.460
and presence to otherwise
invisible entities,

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:41.520
and how sometimes these
objects are understood

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:45.573
to be enlivened, agentive
on their own terms.

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:48.540
In addition to her five monographs,

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:53.070
Dr. Kendall has also edited
and co-edited six volumes.

00:06:53.070 --> 00:06:54.003
It could be more.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:59.190
And also, at the American
Museum of National History,

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:01.350
she has curated several exhibitions

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:03.720
including "Mythic Creatures:

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:08.460
Dragons, Unicorns, and Mermaids" in 2007,

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:11.967
and "Vietnam: Journeys of
Body, Mind, and Spirit."

00:07:13.189 --> 00:07:16.380
And the second exhibition
was a unique collaboration

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:19.740
with the Vietnam Museum of Ethnology

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:23.130
that earned Dr. Kendall a friendship medal

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:25.383
from the Vietnamese government.

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:29.400
And although I have
respect your scholarship

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:31.590
for a long time, this is the first time

00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:34.830
I have had the pleasure
of meeting in person.

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:37.830
In fact, in addition to today's talk,

00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:41.400
Dr. Kendall has kindly agreed
to give a keynote speech

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:45.240
at the upcoming Ewha and
Yale Conference in Seoul,

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:48.570
scheduled for May 26th.

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:51.690
And after the conference,
all the participant,

00:07:51.690 --> 00:07:54.960
including Dr. Kendall and I,

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:58.920
will have a week long trip
in South Korea, field trip,

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:02.370
including places like
Jeju-do, Busan, and Gyeongju.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:05.670
And my colleagues and I
are very looking forward

00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:08.250
to us spending ample time with her.

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:10.500
It's so wonderful to have you here today.

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:13.443
Please join me welcoming
Dr. Laurel Kendall.

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:23.130
- Thank you, Dr. Kim, for
that gracious introduction.

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:26.910
And I consider it a great
honor to be here today

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:30.093
as part of this
distinguished lecture series.

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:36.060
This talk introduces, oops,
sorry, got ahead of myself.

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:39.960
This talk introduces
a comparative project.

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:42.240
I will be looking at Korea.

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:44.640
But not just at Korea.

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:46.923
Vietnam is a part of it,

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:52.350
and there will be a few
words about Myanmar,

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:56.703
and also Bali, Indonesia.

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:05.250
The project resulted in a book

00:09:05.250 --> 00:09:09.330
with Burmese nat spirits on the cover.

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:12.780
It addresses a challenge

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:16.470
posited by anthropologist Webb Keane.

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:21.470
He says, "Here is anthropology.

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:25.230
Here, you have an innately
comparative discipline.

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:28.770
It's been comparative from
the moment of its birth,

00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:33.630
and yet anthropologists today
are absolutely squeamish

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:37.500
about doing overtly comparative work.

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:40.350
We are embarrassed by the inscriptions

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:43.050
of our Victorian forebearers,

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:47.670
and not happy with the
supposedly controlled comparisons

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:52.380
of mid to late 20th century anthropology.

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:55.800
A bid to be scientific with what we're,

00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:59.670
in the end, apples and oranges,

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:04.670
insufficiently contextualized
by their roots and trees."

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:09.450
If I could do anything differently

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:11.940
in the final chapter of my first book,

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:16.020
well, it would probably be to
not write the final chapter,

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:19.260
which is a place where I attempt

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:24.260
to make a gendered comparison
of rituals, gods, ghosts,

00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:30.050
purity and pollution in
Korea, China, and Japan

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:35.360
in ways relatively innocent
of historical, geographical,

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:41.120
and class variation within
Korea, China, and Japan.

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:46.320
It was the big statement I needed to,

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:50.130
at the time, to get a publishing contract.

00:10:50.130 --> 00:10:53.370
But in retrospect, a mistake.

00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:55.920
I will stand by the
rest of the ethnography

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:57.870
between these covers,

00:10:57.870 --> 00:11:01.920
but chapter eight is an embarrassment,

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:04.383
an artifact of another time.

00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:09.690
I have worked with Korean
shamans since the 1970s,

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:12.900
and I have continued to
write books about them.

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:14.790
The project that I am going to share

00:11:14.790 --> 00:11:18.513
with you this evening begins in Korea.

00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:24.693
It begins with mansin who are,

00:11:25.590 --> 00:11:28.470
I would translate them as shamans.

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:31.740
Not just spirit mediums, but shamans,

00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:34.080
master of the spirits.

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:36.810
While a medium could
be considered something

00:11:36.810 --> 00:11:41.810
of the spirit's puppet,
possessed, entranced, taken over,

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:44.527
mansin are agentive.

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:48.690
They are expected to, in
the goodwill of the gods,

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:52.560
to pacify ancestors on
behalf of their clients,

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:55.170
to mollify troublesome spirits,

00:11:55.170 --> 00:11:59.640
and cast things impure away
from their client's path,

00:11:59.640 --> 00:12:04.170
so that is open to
blessing and good fortune.

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:06.870
Their primary work is accomplished

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:09.930
in costumed rituals called kut,

00:12:09.930 --> 00:12:13.650
where they are empowered to make the gods

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:18.150
and ancestors a living
and speaking presence.

00:12:18.150 --> 00:12:22.320
The gods of the shaman's
pantheon are invisible,

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:26.910
but they become a visible
presence in two ways.

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:30.180
One, they are,

00:12:30.180 --> 00:12:34.170
through the mansin's physical
manifestations in kut,

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:36.390
they're mobile, they're vocal,

00:12:36.390 --> 00:12:39.330
but this is a temporary presence.

00:12:39.330 --> 00:12:43.440
They're more permanently
present in the painted images

00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:47.340
that hang above the shaman's altar.

00:12:47.340 --> 00:12:48.750
More constant,

00:12:48.750 --> 00:12:52.623
but as we shall see,
not completely constant.

00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:57.840
The paintings were
backdrop to my early work,

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:00.930
but I did not have
occasion to think very much

00:13:00.930 --> 00:13:04.470
about the images that hung
above the mansin's altar

00:13:04.470 --> 00:13:07.950
beyond understanding that
these were sacred objects,

00:13:07.950 --> 00:13:10.140
that they carried the
identities of the gods

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:12.240
in the shaman's pantheon,

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:15.693
and that they were consequential
for the mansin's work.

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:21.590
I did hear a story about
some paintings of old gods,

00:13:22.050 --> 00:13:24.690
hung behind the altar of the mansin

00:13:24.690 --> 00:13:26.970
I call Yongsu's mother.

00:13:26.970 --> 00:13:29.070
Her sister was also a mansin,

00:13:29.070 --> 00:13:30.900
but her sister wanted to give it up

00:13:30.900 --> 00:13:34.110
because her sister's
son said, "Live with us,

00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:37.200
but you can't be a mansin
if you live with us."

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:39.900
She had dreams. She made prayers.

00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:44.900
It seemed like the gods that
she served were quite willing

00:13:45.420 --> 00:13:48.210
to go over to Yongsu's mother's shrine

00:13:48.210 --> 00:13:51.210
and work with her and her gods.

00:13:51.210 --> 00:13:54.720
Now, the shrine was a very narrow space.

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:58.350
And so, Yongsu's mother
took the new paintings

00:13:58.350 --> 00:14:00.780
and put them under the old paintings.

00:14:00.780 --> 00:14:03.180
This was after they did
all the appropriate ritual.

00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:05.220
And the God said, "Yes,
yes, yes, we'll be here.

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:07.620
We'll work with you. We'll help you."

00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:11.790
And so, she puts them one under the other.

00:14:11.790 --> 00:14:14.760
And then things go south.

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:19.760
She has illnesses. She has
a falling off of business.

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:23.880
Her clients aren't coming
with the usual regularity,

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:25.440
or they're coming for a divination,

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:26.880
then they're going to another mansin

00:14:26.880 --> 00:14:29.823
to have them do the more expensive ritual.

00:14:31.410 --> 00:14:35.130
And she's having trouble
getting a flow of inspiration

00:14:35.130 --> 00:14:38.040
from the gods who are above her altar.

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:40.983
And then she has troubling dreams.

00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:46.880
Now, in one dream, some women
come to her for a divination,

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:49.320
three women.

00:14:49.320 --> 00:14:51.300
And one of them sits down.

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:55.020
The shaman, she does what a mansin does

00:14:55.020 --> 00:14:57.630
when she's about to give an inspiration.

00:14:57.630 --> 00:14:59.580
She brings out a tray.

00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:02.370
There's rice grains on
it. There are coins.

00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:04.530
She's going to throw the rice,

00:15:04.530 --> 00:15:08.070
throw the coins through the configuration.

00:15:08.070 --> 00:15:09.360
She's going to get some sense

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:11.490
of what's going on with the client.

00:15:11.490 --> 00:15:14.100
She'll ask more pointed questions

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:17.073
until she does a full diagnosis.

00:15:18.270 --> 00:15:21.690
She's doing this empowered by the theodicy

00:15:21.690 --> 00:15:23.820
at the divination tray in the picture,

00:15:23.820 --> 00:15:26.400
the Great Spirit grandmother who,

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:29.160
in individual mansin pantheons,

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:34.160
is usually an ancestress
or a teacher shaman,

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:39.240
some shaman in her past who is guiding her

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:41.703
in a lineage type way.

00:15:43.890 --> 00:15:46.020
So, the women come in,

00:15:46.020 --> 00:15:48.357
and one woman sits at the
other side of the tray,

00:15:48.357 --> 00:15:50.820
and the other two, she
says, "Sit down, sit down."

00:15:50.820 --> 00:15:52.470
You know, and they say no.

00:15:52.470 --> 00:15:54.420
They stand by the door.

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:58.410
And she feels very
uncomfortable in the dream,

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:00.870
you know that these women
are just standing scowling.

00:16:00.870 --> 00:16:04.230
And then one of them stalks over,

00:16:04.230 --> 00:16:07.710
takes hold of that tray
and upends it on the floor,

00:16:07.710 --> 00:16:11.250
and she wakes up and she feels really bad

00:16:11.250 --> 00:16:14.340
because this is the rice that belongs

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:16.320
to the Great Spirit grandmother.

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:18.720
And it's like she's been violated.

00:16:18.720 --> 00:16:20.880
Her primary deity has been violated.

00:16:20.880 --> 00:16:25.650
So, she goes right into her
shrine room, and lo and behold,

00:16:25.650 --> 00:16:30.360
the paintings of her own
Great Spirit grandmother

00:16:30.360 --> 00:16:33.900
and the painting of her
sister's primary deity,

00:16:33.900 --> 00:16:34.920
the jade immortal,

00:16:34.920 --> 00:16:38.820
they've stuck together
and fallen to the floor.

00:16:38.820 --> 00:16:41.910
And she says, "They'd been fighting."

00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:44.160
And I thought, fighting? Who's fighting?

00:16:44.160 --> 00:16:47.160
Gods fighting gods.
Paintings fighting paintings.

00:16:47.160 --> 00:16:51.330
Gods fighting each other
through the medium of paintings.

00:16:51.330 --> 00:16:54.720
And I didn't know what was going on.

00:16:54.720 --> 00:16:59.670
And I put the story of the
fallen paintings aside.

00:16:59.670 --> 00:17:02.640
It remained like a nagging itch.

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:04.860
I would come back to it,

00:17:04.860 --> 00:17:09.603
and I'm going to put it aside
for now and come back to it.

00:17:11.610 --> 00:17:16.140
Fast forward and a move
to the north of Vietnam,

00:17:16.140 --> 00:17:18.240
early in the new millennium,

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:21.390
where in my work as a curator at AMNH,

00:17:21.390 --> 00:17:23.430
I had an opportunity to work

00:17:23.430 --> 00:17:26.850
with the Vietnam Museum of Ethnology.

00:17:26.850 --> 00:17:29.887
Now, we were doing an exhibit called

00:17:29.887 --> 00:17:32.760
"Vietnam: Journeys of
Body, Mind, and Spirit,"

00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:34.500
and we had various journeys in it.

00:17:34.500 --> 00:17:37.140
And we thought, "Well, let's
include spirit medium journeys,

00:17:37.140 --> 00:17:40.020
and we can include the
mother goddess tradition,

00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:43.110
which is very rich and colorful."

00:17:43.110 --> 00:17:47.040
And Phu Day Temple, the main
seat of mother goddess worship

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:49.350
in the north of Vietnam,

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:53.880
donated to the museum for the exhibit

00:17:53.880 --> 00:17:58.170
the three statues that
you see in the back there.

00:17:58.170 --> 00:18:02.910
And they saw this as an
attempt to introduce the world

00:18:02.910 --> 00:18:03.930
to their religion,

00:18:03.930 --> 00:18:07.770
which had been suppressed under
anti-superstition campaigns,

00:18:07.770 --> 00:18:12.153
but was back and flourishing
in the new Vietnam.

00:18:13.140 --> 00:18:16.590
And then 9/11 happened.

00:18:16.590 --> 00:18:18.840
And suddenly, you know,

00:18:18.840 --> 00:18:22.530
there was a bit of an economic
crisis in the wake of 9/11.

00:18:22.530 --> 00:18:25.110
The American economy had
actually been sliding

00:18:25.110 --> 00:18:26.730
a little bit before,

00:18:26.730 --> 00:18:29.970
and we were told, "You've gotta
cut your exhibit by a 1/3.

00:18:29.970 --> 00:18:32.130
We just don't have the money."

00:18:32.130 --> 00:18:36.210
And the statues, which
I hadn't really met yet,

00:18:36.210 --> 00:18:39.000
seemed like very good
candidates for cutting,

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:42.510
because they were big, heavy, fragile.

00:18:42.510 --> 00:18:45.420
The only way we could safely
get them to New York would be

00:18:45.420 --> 00:18:48.063
to buy seats for them on the airplane.

00:18:49.440 --> 00:18:54.440
So, the statues were cut.
What do we use in their place?

00:18:54.480 --> 00:18:57.270
And we felt we should consult

00:18:57.270 --> 00:18:59.430
with spirit mediums at Phu Day Temple.

00:18:59.430 --> 00:19:04.020
So, Madam Zwen, the wife
of the shrine keeper,

00:19:04.020 --> 00:19:06.420
very formidable spirit
medium in her own right,

00:19:06.420 --> 00:19:10.320
was going to come and
talk to us at the museum,

00:19:10.320 --> 00:19:13.560
and she was not happy
that we were not going

00:19:13.560 --> 00:19:16.470
to use these spectacular statues.

00:19:16.470 --> 00:19:19.230
Now, the statues had been kept in storage,

00:19:19.230 --> 00:19:20.700
and they'd gotten a little dusty.

00:19:20.700 --> 00:19:23.910
So, the conservators,
meaning to do a good thing,

00:19:23.910 --> 00:19:27.420
took them outside, put
them on a low platform,

00:19:27.420 --> 00:19:28.620
and were dusting them.

00:19:28.620 --> 00:19:30.007
And we were standing around saying,

00:19:30.007 --> 00:19:32.850
"Geez, they're beautiful.
They're so beautiful."

00:19:32.850 --> 00:19:35.100
And Madam Zwen arrives and she walks in,

00:19:35.100 --> 00:19:37.567
she sees the conservators
at work, and she says,

00:19:37.567 --> 00:19:39.270
"You put them on the floor?

00:19:39.270 --> 00:19:41.910
Would you put Ho Chi Minh on the floor?

00:19:41.910 --> 00:19:43.560
How can you do that?"

00:19:43.560 --> 00:19:47.850
And profuse apologies and sued.

00:19:47.850 --> 00:19:50.970
Now, my colleagues were
surprised by her reaction

00:19:50.970 --> 00:19:55.350
because they understood,
as I did not understand,

00:19:55.350 --> 00:19:57.810
that holy images like these statues,

00:19:57.810 --> 00:20:00.810
in a temple setting,
they would be animated

00:20:00.810 --> 00:20:04.920
with the spirit essence
of the mother goddesses,

00:20:04.920 --> 00:20:06.810
or a Buddha, or whatever,

00:20:06.810 --> 00:20:09.240
but that these had not been animated.

00:20:09.240 --> 00:20:13.230
That they were, as they
understood it, just statues.

00:20:13.230 --> 00:20:16.740
And yet, no, there were
something more than just statues,

00:20:16.740 --> 00:20:18.573
and we wanted to find out.

00:20:20.730 --> 00:20:24.900
Now, the mother goddess tradition
of Vietnam interested me

00:20:24.900 --> 00:20:29.763
as it interests anyone who
knows Korean shaman practice.

00:20:31.260 --> 00:20:34.410
Because again, you see
the elaborate costumes,

00:20:34.410 --> 00:20:37.380
you see the sequential
appearance of the gods,

00:20:37.380 --> 00:20:41.130
you see a mimicking of, you know,

00:20:41.130 --> 00:20:44.280
royal dress and hierarchies of gods

00:20:44.280 --> 00:20:47.010
that parallel temporal hierarchies

00:20:47.010 --> 00:20:50.130
of kings and officials and so on.

00:20:50.130 --> 00:20:52.863
So, I really wanted to
learn more about it.

00:20:54.840 --> 00:20:59.840
And in 2004, thanks to the
Wenner-Gren Foundation,

00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:02.760
my Vietnamese colleagues and I were able

00:21:02.760 --> 00:21:04.863
to do a further study.

00:21:06.157 --> 00:21:09.240
Vu Thi Thanh Tam and Nguyen Thu Thi Huong

00:21:09.240 --> 00:21:13.710
and I went back to Phu Day Temple

00:21:13.710 --> 00:21:18.000
and talked to the temple
keeper and talked to,

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:19.410
you know, about, "Tell us,

00:21:19.410 --> 00:21:21.990
tell us what these statues meant,

00:21:21.990 --> 00:21:26.990
and tell us why it was so
wrong to put them low down

00:21:27.750 --> 00:21:30.210
to try to clean them when
our poor conservators

00:21:30.210 --> 00:21:32.670
thought they were doing a good work."

00:21:32.670 --> 00:21:35.670
And then we went beyond Phu Day.

00:21:35.670 --> 00:21:39.000
We talked to other mediums.
We talked to carvers.

00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:41.520
We talked to a lacquer maker.

00:21:41.520 --> 00:21:45.510
We talked to people who
sold statues in the market.

00:21:45.510 --> 00:21:48.630
And at the start of many interviews,

00:21:48.630 --> 00:21:51.150
just to explain what we were about,

00:21:51.150 --> 00:21:54.300
I told the story of the paintings in Korea

00:21:54.300 --> 00:21:57.570
that had fought with each
other and fallen to the floor.

00:21:57.570 --> 00:22:00.180
And this was a very good move

00:22:00.180 --> 00:22:03.750
because this banished
any perception that me,

00:22:03.750 --> 00:22:07.080
as an American, was going to
look down as superstitious,

00:22:07.080 --> 00:22:09.483
all this stuff that was going on.

00:22:10.740 --> 00:22:14.550
And that I was open to the idea
that images could do things

00:22:14.550 --> 00:22:17.520
like fight with each
other and cause bad luck.

00:22:17.520 --> 00:22:22.520
And this was the beginning of
my education in magical images

00:22:22.530 --> 00:22:25.530
and the start of a comparative project

00:22:25.530 --> 00:22:29.640
that would eventually
draw in Myanmar and Bali,

00:22:29.640 --> 00:22:34.470
and draw me back to shaman
shrines in South Korea

00:22:34.470 --> 00:22:37.863
and the matter of the fallen paintings.

00:22:38.730 --> 00:22:43.470
In Vietnam, we were interviewing
carvers, spirit mediums,

00:22:43.470 --> 00:22:47.100
ritual masters, et cetera
at a very good moment.

00:22:47.100 --> 00:22:50.430
Because under high socialism,

00:22:50.430 --> 00:22:53.250
there had been many acts of iconoclasm.

00:22:53.250 --> 00:22:57.600
The consequences were very
fresh in people's minds.

00:22:57.600 --> 00:22:59.520
You know, they threw
the statues in the pond

00:22:59.520 --> 00:23:01.273
and somebody died in a flood.

00:23:01.273 --> 00:23:03.847
And this woman who was
telling me this says,

00:23:03.847 --> 00:23:06.150
"And I have chronic arthritis, you know,

00:23:06.150 --> 00:23:09.630
if only we hadn't done
that to the statues."

00:23:09.630 --> 00:23:12.060
Also, the market was back.

00:23:12.060 --> 00:23:13.770
Things were looking up,

00:23:13.770 --> 00:23:17.430
and there was a felt need to repair,

00:23:17.430 --> 00:23:19.800
to restore the fallen statues,

00:23:19.800 --> 00:23:22.200
to repair decaying temples.

00:23:22.200 --> 00:23:26.880
So, statue carving was very
much on people's minds.

00:23:26.880 --> 00:23:31.650
Now, all of my life, all of my adult life,

00:23:31.650 --> 00:23:35.730
I have been bowing to
Buddhas showing respect.

00:23:35.730 --> 00:23:40.173
I've been guided in my
form by many Korean women.

00:23:42.030 --> 00:23:45.000
I never realized that
the Buddha was supposed

00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:50.000
to really be there until
I began doing this work.

00:23:52.170 --> 00:23:57.170
I was learning what my Vietnamese
colleagues knew very well,

00:23:57.870 --> 00:23:59.640
that through ritual acts,

00:23:59.640 --> 00:24:03.660
the statues had been made
into social entities.

00:24:03.660 --> 00:24:07.320
After being ceremoniously
placed on the altar,

00:24:07.320 --> 00:24:10.140
the cavity in the statue
body would be filled

00:24:10.140 --> 00:24:12.150
with animating matter,

00:24:12.150 --> 00:24:16.710
sometimes regarded as an
approximation of human organs,

00:24:16.710 --> 00:24:19.350
amulets, and other things.

00:24:19.350 --> 00:24:21.240
And then on a lucky day,

00:24:21.240 --> 00:24:24.240
in the dark midnight hour of the rat,

00:24:24.240 --> 00:24:29.240
a ritual master or a monk
would bring the statue to life,

00:24:29.850 --> 00:24:34.290
awakening its senses, opening its eyes.

00:24:34.290 --> 00:24:37.170
Now, we studied the, you know,

00:24:37.170 --> 00:24:38.880
what do you do to make the statue alive?

00:24:38.880 --> 00:24:43.880
But we also, studied the
protocols for making the statue

00:24:44.100 --> 00:24:48.330
what the carvers and the spirit
mediums considered necessary

00:24:48.330 --> 00:24:50.640
to make a powerful statue,

00:24:50.640 --> 00:24:53.490
and then the statue's subsequent carriers.

00:24:53.490 --> 00:24:55.978
When I searched the literature,

00:24:55.978 --> 00:25:00.720
studies from India,
from China, from Japan,

00:25:00.720 --> 00:25:05.720
I was surprised by how widespread
these ideas seemed to be,

00:25:06.450 --> 00:25:10.770
that the images transformed
by ritual action into presence

00:25:10.770 --> 00:25:13.920
and that the presence
becomes consequential.

00:25:13.920 --> 00:25:17.790
And I was tempted into
further comparative work,

00:25:17.790 --> 00:25:19.830
building my own questions

00:25:19.830 --> 00:25:24.063
upon an existing scaffold of writing.

00:25:24.990 --> 00:25:29.990
In Myanmar, I joined
forces with Erin Hasinoff,

00:25:30.030 --> 00:25:33.330
and AMNH research associate
who had been working there

00:25:33.330 --> 00:25:38.330
with field assistants Pyi
Pyi Thant and Thei Thein Win,

00:25:38.700 --> 00:25:41.520
to interview spirit mediums and carvers.

00:25:41.520 --> 00:25:44.610
This was a very, very brief incident,

00:25:44.610 --> 00:25:48.360
but it helped to round out the picture.

00:25:48.360 --> 00:25:50.070
And then in Bali,

00:25:50.070 --> 00:25:52.350
I have had the pleasure of working

00:25:52.350 --> 00:25:55.080
with Dr. Ni Wayan Pasek Ariati,

00:25:55.080 --> 00:25:57.390
a scholar of religious history,

00:25:57.390 --> 00:26:01.050
and a veteran field worker
who will also be participating

00:26:01.050 --> 00:26:03.993
in the Ewha-Yale conference
just to bring everything home.

00:26:06.060 --> 00:26:11.060
In Korea, I have usually been
a solitary anthropologist,

00:26:11.430 --> 00:26:14.280
working sometimes with a field assistant,

00:26:14.280 --> 00:26:17.310
but I was learning the
pleasures of teamwork,

00:26:17.310 --> 00:26:18.780
especially when different people

00:26:18.780 --> 00:26:21.780
have different kinds of expertise.

00:26:21.780 --> 00:26:26.250
And when I returned to the
question of the fallen paintings,

00:26:26.250 --> 00:26:31.230
I joined forces with Dr.
Jongsung Yang, a folklorist,

00:26:31.230 --> 00:26:34.320
and Dr. Yul-soo Yoon, an art historian,

00:26:34.320 --> 00:26:38.040
both of whom have had interesting things

00:26:38.040 --> 00:26:40.443
to say about shaman paintings.

00:26:43.110 --> 00:26:45.690
Both of them now have private museums

00:26:45.690 --> 00:26:50.690
with extensive collections and ties in...

00:26:51.000 --> 00:26:54.360
You know, they complimented my ties.

00:26:54.360 --> 00:26:58.200
All of these collaborations
produced co-authored papers

00:26:58.200 --> 00:27:00.990
in English language professional journals,

00:27:00.990 --> 00:27:04.890
and a co-authored book
on shaman paintings.

00:27:04.890 --> 00:27:09.570
These were cited pieces
in the patchwork quilt

00:27:09.570 --> 00:27:13.320
that would become "Mediums
and Magical Things,"

00:27:13.320 --> 00:27:16.320
the 2021 book.

00:27:16.320 --> 00:27:19.560
And because it had worked
so well for me in Vietnam,

00:27:19.560 --> 00:27:22.380
when I met with carvers in Myanmar,

00:27:22.380 --> 00:27:24.450
I would use the story of the paintings

00:27:24.450 --> 00:27:26.910
that had fought with each
other and fell to the floor

00:27:26.910 --> 00:27:29.580
in my conversations there,

00:27:29.580 --> 00:27:33.900
and also, with carvers in Bali.

00:27:33.900 --> 00:27:36.300
I came to imagine this project

00:27:36.300 --> 00:27:39.960
as a wide ranging vicarious dialogue

00:27:39.960 --> 00:27:43.590
between conversation
partners in different places

00:27:43.590 --> 00:27:46.650
who have never met face to face.

00:27:46.650 --> 00:27:49.440
A carver in Vietnam told
me this, I would say.

00:27:49.440 --> 00:27:52.440
A mask vendor in Bali told me that.

00:27:52.440 --> 00:27:54.420
In a South Korean shaman shrine,

00:27:54.420 --> 00:27:58.080
the paintings fought with each
other and fell to the floor.

00:27:58.080 --> 00:28:02.247
I approached this work
through three affordances.

00:28:02.247 --> 00:28:04.620
Affordances, like, like, you know,

00:28:04.620 --> 00:28:07.710
you think of the handle on the coffee cup,

00:28:07.710 --> 00:28:09.870
there are all different
kinds of coffee cups,

00:28:09.870 --> 00:28:11.373
all different shapes,

00:28:12.510 --> 00:28:15.810
but they're, you know, a
simple single operation

00:28:15.810 --> 00:28:20.045
that helps bring the cup to one's lips.

00:28:20.045 --> 00:28:24.570
And that's how I approached
different ways people had

00:28:24.570 --> 00:28:29.570
of talking about how they
make and enliven these images.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:33.420
Birgit Meyer from the field
of material religious studies

00:28:33.420 --> 00:28:36.090
calls for a deeper appreciation

00:28:36.090 --> 00:28:39.360
of the process of
fabricating religious things.

00:28:39.360 --> 00:28:42.780
And this is my first affordance

00:28:42.780 --> 00:28:46.860
of most direct interest for a conversation

00:28:46.860 --> 00:28:51.600
about active matter and
its spiritual implications.

00:28:51.600 --> 00:28:55.020
And this is an affordance that spans

00:28:55.020 --> 00:28:58.290
what we might think of as the
Catholic world originating,

00:28:58.290 --> 00:29:00.570
but no longer by any means restricted

00:29:00.570 --> 00:29:04.080
to Europe and the East, and you know,

00:29:04.080 --> 00:29:07.500
but Europe and the Eastern
Mediterranean originally,

00:29:07.500 --> 00:29:10.770
and the Hindu Buddhist world, you know,

00:29:10.770 --> 00:29:14.910
way across all of Eurasia, more or less.

00:29:14.910 --> 00:29:19.910
Throughout this vast
Eurasian geographic span,

00:29:19.920 --> 00:29:23.520
we find places where religious images

00:29:23.520 --> 00:29:28.380
have long traditions
of workshop production,

00:29:28.380 --> 00:29:29.940
where they have been commissioned

00:29:29.940 --> 00:29:33.300
as acts of conspicuous devotion

00:29:33.300 --> 00:29:36.840
and produced to a higher level of skill,

00:29:36.840 --> 00:29:40.440
where levels of material
consumption enable standards

00:29:40.440 --> 00:29:42.600
and hierarchies of value

00:29:42.600 --> 00:29:46.470
with respect to materials and craft.

00:29:46.470 --> 00:29:48.870
Those who commission install,

00:29:48.870 --> 00:29:53.760
and subsequently venerate
sacred images are dependent,

00:29:53.760 --> 00:29:55.830
or, well, in the Catholic word,

00:29:55.830 --> 00:29:57.390
we don't say venerate the images,

00:29:57.390 --> 00:30:00.480
but you know, offer prayers and sincerity

00:30:00.480 --> 00:30:02.853
inspired by the statues.

00:30:04.500 --> 00:30:08.400
But all right, in both
instances, the Catholic world,

00:30:08.400 --> 00:30:11.430
the Hindu Buddhist world,
in the first instance,

00:30:11.430 --> 00:30:13.680
these things are being made in workshops.

00:30:13.680 --> 00:30:15.390
They're not, you're not fabricating

00:30:15.390 --> 00:30:19.080
your individual personal religious object.

00:30:19.080 --> 00:30:23.520
And the critical work
then has been entrusted

00:30:23.520 --> 00:30:25.533
to other hands.

00:30:26.610 --> 00:30:29.640
But, and this is my second affordance,

00:30:29.640 --> 00:30:33.003
now we slice that first
affordance in half.

00:30:34.110 --> 00:30:37.470
Significant difference
between religious statues

00:30:37.470 --> 00:30:39.390
in a Catholic tradition

00:30:39.390 --> 00:30:43.800
and religious statues in a
Hindu or a Buddhist tradition,

00:30:43.800 --> 00:30:46.293
appreciating that these
are multiple traditions.

00:30:47.250 --> 00:30:51.540
Catholic images are
considered sacramentals.

00:30:51.540 --> 00:30:53.430
They inspire prayer.

00:30:53.430 --> 00:30:56.100
They enable the reception of grace,

00:30:56.100 --> 00:31:00.570
but they are not or are
not supposed to be idols.

00:31:00.570 --> 00:31:03.990
When they show signs of
life, they're considered,

00:31:03.990 --> 00:31:05.490
you know, this is a weird thing.

00:31:05.490 --> 00:31:10.490
This is miraculous and or
theologically problematic.

00:31:10.980 --> 00:31:15.980
And instances of statues
weeping, flying around,

00:31:16.170 --> 00:31:21.170
sweating, inspire very serious
religious investigation.

00:31:22.290 --> 00:31:24.240
In the Hindu Buddhist world,

00:31:24.240 --> 00:31:29.240
images are made to be
inhabited by gods or Buddhist,

00:31:29.790 --> 00:31:34.790
as you saw in the slide of
the Vietnamese carver at work,

00:31:35.400 --> 00:31:37.950
and there's a cavity in the back.

00:31:37.950 --> 00:31:40.320
Images in the Hindu Buddhist world

00:31:40.320 --> 00:31:42.750
are regarded as presences,

00:31:42.750 --> 00:31:46.020
consecrated and venerated
through appropriate techniques

00:31:46.020 --> 00:31:49.920
and practices, sacred,
powerful, sometimes dangerous,

00:31:49.920 --> 00:31:54.920
but also, material objects that
will deteriorate over time.

00:31:55.440 --> 00:31:56.550
In these places,

00:31:56.550 --> 00:32:00.450
the relative quality of
materials, of craftsmanship,

00:32:00.450 --> 00:32:03.540
and some practices we might cautiously,

00:32:03.540 --> 00:32:07.530
and heuristically call
magic are consequential

00:32:07.530 --> 00:32:10.110
for the image's subsequent career

00:32:10.110 --> 00:32:13.260
as an unsouled and agentive thing,

00:32:13.260 --> 00:32:18.260
consequential for its
efficacy or absence thereof.

00:32:20.070 --> 00:32:24.580
The final affordance in my 4K studies

00:32:25.740 --> 00:32:30.030
that fall under the very broad
umbrella of sacred images

00:32:30.030 --> 00:32:35.030
in a larger Asian world are
those distinctive traditions

00:32:35.190 --> 00:32:40.190
of mediumship and showmanship
that work in conjunction

00:32:40.350 --> 00:32:44.490
with the gods or spirits
that possess spirit mediums

00:32:44.490 --> 00:32:48.900
and are manifested or are
manifested by shamans.

00:32:48.900 --> 00:32:52.080
A doubling of the well-crafted image

00:32:52.080 --> 00:32:55.560
and the mobile body as in a Korean kut.

00:32:55.560 --> 00:32:57.180
So, three affordances.

00:32:57.180 --> 00:33:02.040
There's workshop production
to a high degree of quality.

00:33:02.040 --> 00:33:07.020
There's the notion that
the statue contains,

00:33:07.020 --> 00:33:09.810
is made to be ensouled,

00:33:09.810 --> 00:33:12.300
can be ensouled through ritual practices,

00:33:12.300 --> 00:33:14.580
can also be de-ensouled.

00:33:14.580 --> 00:33:18.960
And finally, there are specific traditions

00:33:18.960 --> 00:33:21.420
where the images work in conjunction

00:33:21.420 --> 00:33:23.430
with a shaman or a medium.

00:33:23.430 --> 00:33:26.433
We'll say something about how and why.

00:33:27.300 --> 00:33:30.933
Okay, this happens in Len Dong in Vietnam.

00:33:32.130 --> 00:33:37.020
This happens in Nat Pwe in Myanmar.

00:33:37.020 --> 00:33:42.020
And in the Balinese, the temple festivals,

00:33:42.210 --> 00:33:45.690
the mask image covers and subsumes

00:33:45.690 --> 00:33:47.940
the identity of the medium,

00:33:47.940 --> 00:33:50.940
a blending of image and mobile body

00:33:50.940 --> 00:33:54.420
when the deity appears
in temple festivals.

00:33:54.420 --> 00:33:56.640
My work, I want to emphasize,

00:33:56.640 --> 00:34:00.450
thinking of that unfortunate
chapter eight of my first book,

00:34:00.450 --> 00:34:04.470
this is not going to be
a controlled comparison.

00:34:04.470 --> 00:34:09.470
As will become evident, it
was the unexpected answers

00:34:09.510 --> 00:34:13.683
to otherwise legible questions
that made things interesting.

00:34:14.640 --> 00:34:19.050
In the carving workshops of
the Red River Delta of Vietnam,

00:34:19.050 --> 00:34:21.900
I learned the importance of craft.

00:34:21.900 --> 00:34:24.000
Using properly seasoned wood

00:34:24.000 --> 00:34:26.250
and working it with a knowing hand,

00:34:26.250 --> 00:34:27.930
a knowledge of tradition,

00:34:27.930 --> 00:34:32.670
a clear vision in the mind's
eye to make a proper image,

00:34:32.670 --> 00:34:34.350
I think thus far, you know,

00:34:34.350 --> 00:34:37.320
any sculptor would probably say that,

00:34:37.320 --> 00:34:40.590
not just good wood and
excellent skill though,

00:34:40.590 --> 00:34:43.380
but in the most traditional workshops,

00:34:43.380 --> 00:34:47.580
attentions to things,
we might consider magic.

00:34:47.580 --> 00:34:50.940
The wood is cut on an
auspicious day and hours

00:34:50.940 --> 00:34:53.130
set by the lunar almanac

00:34:53.130 --> 00:34:58.130
after sending out any ghosts
or infelicitous elements

00:34:58.590 --> 00:35:02.160
that might have taken up
residents inside the old wood.

00:35:02.160 --> 00:35:05.850
And the carver who makes
the cuts is endowed

00:35:05.850 --> 00:35:08.340
with a horoscope that sinks properly

00:35:08.340 --> 00:35:10.770
with the year, day, month, and hour.

00:35:10.770 --> 00:35:13.740
The wood is cut to an auspicious measure

00:35:13.740 --> 00:35:15.990
on the scheme attributed to Lu Ban,

00:35:15.990 --> 00:35:18.480
the Chinese God of carpentry.

00:35:18.480 --> 00:35:22.020
The master carver seeks a
vision of the intended deity

00:35:22.020 --> 00:35:25.800
in his own mind's eye
before he begins to carve.

00:35:25.800 --> 00:35:29.820
He makes his workers observe
various workshop taboos.

00:35:29.820 --> 00:35:33.060
No bare chests. No inauspicious talk.

00:35:33.060 --> 00:35:35.520
No laundry hanging over the statue.

00:35:35.520 --> 00:35:38.280
No menstruating women touching the statue.

00:35:38.280 --> 00:35:39.960
No dog meat consumed.

00:35:39.960 --> 00:35:42.180
No garlic or strong smelling herbs

00:35:42.180 --> 00:35:44.760
when the final gilding is applied.

00:35:44.760 --> 00:35:49.760
Offerings and petitions
punctuate the entire process

00:35:50.370 --> 00:35:52.740
from the master carvers offerings

00:35:52.740 --> 00:35:56.050
to the goddess before going
to discuss the commission

00:35:57.180 --> 00:36:01.560
with a potential patron to
the patron's final inspection

00:36:01.560 --> 00:36:03.600
of the finished statue,

00:36:03.600 --> 00:36:06.120
and request of the goddess's permission

00:36:06.120 --> 00:36:08.073
to bring it to the home temple.

00:36:11.820 --> 00:36:13.350
In this process,

00:36:13.350 --> 00:36:16.860
it becomes difficult
to parse the difference

00:36:16.860 --> 00:36:20.040
between the strictly material practices

00:36:20.040 --> 00:36:23.730
and things done for other
reasons, other consequences.

00:36:23.730 --> 00:36:27.480
And I want to note when
I speak about magic

00:36:27.480 --> 00:36:30.630
in Vietnam or Korea or anywhere,

00:36:30.630 --> 00:36:34.410
these places are not
strangers to modern science.

00:36:34.410 --> 00:36:36.780
This is something else.

00:36:36.780 --> 00:36:40.909
The statues like the communion
host are material things

00:36:40.909 --> 00:36:45.909
whose intended work
overflows material logic.

00:36:46.080 --> 00:36:49.770
A statue will be less
efficacious, even inauspicious,

00:36:49.770 --> 00:36:53.640
where the carver substitutes
cheap branch wood

00:36:53.640 --> 00:36:56.340
for more durable core wood,

00:36:56.340 --> 00:36:59.700
where improperly seasoned wood rots

00:36:59.700 --> 00:37:02.910
or becomes infested with insects,

00:37:02.910 --> 00:37:06.720
where the artisan skimps
on the gold lacquer

00:37:06.720 --> 00:37:08.820
commissioned by the patron,

00:37:08.820 --> 00:37:12.093
or where the product is simply ugly.

00:37:13.230 --> 00:37:16.320
If human beings do not punish the carver,

00:37:16.320 --> 00:37:18.990
the god's will said one master carver

00:37:18.990 --> 00:37:20.700
who considers his own work,

00:37:20.700 --> 00:37:23.940
his scrupulous attention
to all these details

00:37:23.940 --> 00:37:27.000
as doing the mother goddess's work.

00:37:27.000 --> 00:37:31.980
Of course, not all images
are equally efficacious,

00:37:31.980 --> 00:37:34.950
nor are they all made with the same care.

00:37:34.950 --> 00:37:38.428
A shopkeeper on Hanoi's Hang Quat Street,

00:37:38.428 --> 00:37:43.428
hive for these activities,
described a hierarchy of value.

00:37:43.890 --> 00:37:46.800
She said the least expensive

00:37:46.800 --> 00:37:50.700
and the least efficacious
in her view, statues,

00:37:50.700 --> 00:37:53.280
are the ones that are
just sort of chunked out.

00:37:53.280 --> 00:37:55.740
You know, so, many types
of the Tenth Prince,

00:37:55.740 --> 00:37:56.790
and they're all on the shelf

00:37:56.790 --> 00:37:58.563
and you come in and you buy one.

00:37:59.580 --> 00:38:03.690
The next are commissioned
through the shopkeeper.

00:38:03.690 --> 00:38:05.580
Yeah, I want a Tenth Prince.

00:38:05.580 --> 00:38:06.630
And they're made to a...

00:38:06.630 --> 00:38:09.270
They're more expensive.
They're made a little better.

00:38:09.270 --> 00:38:11.640
They might work a little better, she said.

00:38:11.640 --> 00:38:16.640
And then the best are the
statues made by a master carver,

00:38:16.890 --> 00:38:19.260
using all of the traditional processes

00:38:19.260 --> 00:38:21.300
I've been talking about.

00:38:21.300 --> 00:38:25.650
And these of course are the most expensive

00:38:25.650 --> 00:38:27.840
and the most efficacious.

00:38:27.840 --> 00:38:30.810
Statues, like those that had been gifted

00:38:30.810 --> 00:38:34.260
to the Vietnam Museum of Ethnology,

00:38:34.260 --> 00:38:37.890
had been made according to
all the procedures for making,

00:38:37.890 --> 00:38:39.420
but the process had stopped there.

00:38:39.420 --> 00:38:42.183
They then were not ensouled,
but they could have been.

00:38:43.110 --> 00:38:47.493
Younger mediums use
less expensive statues.

00:38:48.840 --> 00:38:52.091
And they will say, "Oh,
statues are statue.

00:38:52.091 --> 00:38:56.250
It's the ritual master
who puts the god inside.

00:38:56.250 --> 00:38:59.010
You just need a good ritual master."

00:38:59.010 --> 00:39:02.880
But the older mediums associated
with established temples

00:39:02.880 --> 00:39:07.650
will say, "Oh, that's naive.
That's even dangerous."

00:39:07.650 --> 00:39:12.180
Assuming that badly made
statues are not ling,

00:39:12.180 --> 00:39:15.090
do not secure the mother goddess's favor

00:39:15.090 --> 00:39:18.093
and will bring inauspicious consequences.

00:39:19.080 --> 00:39:22.230
I would find this kind of argument

00:39:22.230 --> 00:39:23.490
about what you have to do.

00:39:23.490 --> 00:39:28.490
No, you don't have to do
that in other places as well.

00:39:28.530 --> 00:39:32.700
And I see this as, this is
kind of a dialogic practice.

00:39:32.700 --> 00:39:35.910
It is important to point
out the disagreements

00:39:35.910 --> 00:39:40.170
that living people have when
it's a living tradition.

00:39:40.170 --> 00:39:45.170
And this is a symptom
of how real it all is.

00:39:45.300 --> 00:39:49.440
Where the marketplace offers
a range of market choices,

00:39:49.440 --> 00:39:52.233
even for the images of gods.

00:39:53.400 --> 00:39:58.400
Now, Myanmar is also
in the Buddhist world.

00:39:58.800 --> 00:40:02.730
And albeit Theravada, not Mahayana,

00:40:02.730 --> 00:40:05.706
and with a different ritual calendar.

00:40:05.706 --> 00:40:10.440
But from what I had read
before going there in 2011,

00:40:10.440 --> 00:40:12.960
images are also animated.

00:40:12.960 --> 00:40:17.010
Nat spirits are invested with
the nats, butterfly soul,

00:40:17.010 --> 00:40:20.280
and live in close association
with spirit mediums

00:40:20.280 --> 00:40:23.823
who carefully tend them,
bathe them, dress them.

00:40:24.720 --> 00:40:26.610
Note the arrangement in the basket.

00:40:26.610 --> 00:40:28.770
They've arrived at the festival,

00:40:28.770 --> 00:40:32.340
and now the statues have not
been piled inside the basket,

00:40:32.340 --> 00:40:34.260
which would be more protective,

00:40:34.260 --> 00:40:36.060
would also be an economy of space.

00:40:36.060 --> 00:40:38.220
No, the nats should see where we're going.

00:40:38.220 --> 00:40:40.113
So, they're out, you know.

00:40:45.720 --> 00:40:48.780
I had been curious about
the Burmese nat play

00:40:48.780 --> 00:40:50.220
for a very long time.

00:40:50.220 --> 00:40:53.460
Again, this is another
kind of ritual where,

00:40:53.460 --> 00:40:56.820
like the Korean shaman's
kut, like the Len Dong,

00:40:56.820 --> 00:40:58.980
it's richly costumed,

00:40:58.980 --> 00:41:03.330
the gods appearing in this
sequence of incarnation

00:41:03.330 --> 00:41:07.620
that accompanies costuming
and departure decostuming,

00:41:07.620 --> 00:41:10.440
and on through a sequence.

00:41:10.440 --> 00:41:12.990
I wanted to see, in terms of this project,

00:41:12.990 --> 00:41:15.330
if the workshops that carved the medium

00:41:15.330 --> 00:41:20.330
small wooden images, were
attentive to both magic and craft

00:41:22.260 --> 00:41:25.233
as their counterparts in Vietnam might be.

00:41:26.250 --> 00:41:31.080
I learned that, in Myanmar,
mediums carry their own images

00:41:31.080 --> 00:41:36.080
with them to rituals as
their co-present enablers

00:41:36.480 --> 00:41:39.510
and to festivals in
the nat's home temples,

00:41:39.510 --> 00:41:43.110
where the little images
are periodically irradiated

00:41:43.110 --> 00:41:45.990
by their presence to the source statues,

00:41:45.990 --> 00:41:48.603
the original seats of the nat.

00:41:50.460 --> 00:41:55.460
This is a more portable
practice than the immobile stat.

00:41:55.800 --> 00:41:59.640
In Vietnam, the ritual
master sets it on the altar.

00:41:59.640 --> 00:42:03.780
And if, you know, one medium
told me she was dusting

00:42:03.780 --> 00:42:05.460
and she knocked it a little askew,

00:42:05.460 --> 00:42:09.270
and she had pain in her
arms and so on and so forth

00:42:09.270 --> 00:42:12.630
until she got a ritual master
and just set it right again.

00:42:12.630 --> 00:42:14.520
But no, these are quite mobile.

00:42:14.520 --> 00:42:19.520
They go with the, nat draw the mediums,

00:42:20.910 --> 00:42:23.103
and they get a bit banged up.

00:42:24.270 --> 00:42:26.670
Ethnographies of Myanmar described

00:42:26.670 --> 00:42:28.200
their battered condition.

00:42:28.200 --> 00:42:31.350
I didn't see batter. I saw, as in Vietnam,

00:42:31.350 --> 00:42:35.700
a moment where people could
afford to regild their images

00:42:35.700 --> 00:42:39.180
and things were looking pretty good.

00:42:39.180 --> 00:42:41.970
When we spoke to the carvers

00:42:41.970 --> 00:42:45.120
near the Mahamuni Pagoda in Mandalay

00:42:45.120 --> 00:42:48.330
and the Shwedagon Pagoda in Yangon,

00:42:48.330 --> 00:42:52.080
carvers were familiar with our questions.

00:42:52.080 --> 00:42:55.230
Yes, the carver should
abstain from alcohol

00:42:55.230 --> 00:42:58.950
and work with a clean body
and a respectful attitude,

00:42:58.950 --> 00:43:02.940
shoes removed as in a
pagoda or a monastery.

00:43:02.940 --> 00:43:06.870
And yes, the statues must
be beautifully carved

00:43:06.870 --> 00:43:09.690
according to a proper prototype.

00:43:09.690 --> 00:43:12.750
A ritual offering might
mark the start of a large

00:43:12.750 --> 00:43:14.100
and important commission.

00:43:14.100 --> 00:43:16.560
And depending on the
client's willingness to pay,

00:43:16.560 --> 00:43:20.310
a carver would observe different
levels of Buddhist precept.

00:43:20.310 --> 00:43:23.910
But we soon realized that our interest

00:43:23.910 --> 00:43:28.110
in nat images was
confusing to the carvers,

00:43:28.110 --> 00:43:29.760
the carvers we were talking to

00:43:29.760 --> 00:43:31.770
who considered the nat a sideline.

00:43:31.770 --> 00:43:36.240
The Buddha images were where
it was really happening.

00:43:36.240 --> 00:43:40.980
And when they described
prohibitions and purifications,

00:43:40.980 --> 00:43:44.640
they were largely
referencing carving a Buddha

00:43:44.640 --> 00:43:47.490
for a major temple commission.

00:43:47.490 --> 00:43:48.900
In addition,

00:43:48.900 --> 00:43:53.340
the form of a Buddha image
demands strict adherence

00:43:53.340 --> 00:43:57.630
to precise protocols set down in texts.

00:43:57.630 --> 00:44:01.860
While not images, need only
approximate the gestures

00:44:01.860 --> 00:44:04.920
and costumes deployed by mediums.

00:44:04.920 --> 00:44:08.130
And sometimes, a medium will see the nat

00:44:08.130 --> 00:44:10.350
in a particular gesture in a dream,

00:44:10.350 --> 00:44:12.813
and that becomes the gesture of the image.

00:44:15.000 --> 00:44:18.720
And one carver said he felt
happier when carving the Buddha

00:44:18.720 --> 00:44:21.573
because he could focus
his whole mind on it.

00:44:22.410 --> 00:44:26.070
The nats, he said, could
be rougher and cruder

00:44:26.070 --> 00:44:27.674
than a well-finished Buddha image.

00:44:27.674 --> 00:44:32.490
I have since heard that
there is a special fraternity

00:44:32.490 --> 00:44:37.490
of nat carvers who do take
the carving very seriously,

00:44:37.920 --> 00:44:42.360
but they're a small, small
corner on the supply.

00:44:42.360 --> 00:44:43.530
It's like in Vietnam,

00:44:43.530 --> 00:44:45.690
are you going to buy
a very elegant statue?

00:44:45.690 --> 00:44:47.940
Are you going to buy a lesser?

00:44:47.940 --> 00:44:52.860
But there seemed also to
be a bit of a difference

00:44:52.860 --> 00:44:57.030
between mother goddess figures in Vietnam

00:44:57.030 --> 00:45:00.420
and nat figures in Myanmar.

00:45:00.420 --> 00:45:04.413
There's such a very clear hierarchization.

00:45:05.610 --> 00:45:08.580
The Buddha is absolutely central,

00:45:08.580 --> 00:45:12.693
and the nats are something else again.

00:45:13.600 --> 00:45:18.600
Nats are relative to the
Buddha, that they died,

00:45:20.400 --> 00:45:22.200
most of them died violently.

00:45:22.200 --> 00:45:25.740
They died through treachery
or otherwise badly.

00:45:25.740 --> 00:45:30.150
These are not spiritual
people or spiritual entities.

00:45:30.150 --> 00:45:34.200
In dying, they were unable to
assume a prayerful orientation

00:45:34.200 --> 00:45:35.910
toward the next life.

00:45:35.910 --> 00:45:38.760
They were denied reincarnation.

00:45:38.760 --> 00:45:41.220
As Burma scholars described them,

00:45:41.220 --> 00:45:44.070
nats remain trapped in the emotions

00:45:44.070 --> 00:45:47.790
and appetites they bore while alive.

00:45:47.790 --> 00:45:50.970
While where good Buddhists
work towards salvation

00:45:50.970 --> 00:45:53.490
by periodically following precepts,

00:45:53.490 --> 00:45:55.740
enjoining moral behavior,

00:45:55.740 --> 00:45:58.320
fasting, temperance, and other austerity,

00:45:58.320 --> 00:46:03.180
the nats exemplify the appetites
and failings of the flesh

00:46:03.180 --> 00:46:05.130
in the stories told about them,

00:46:05.130 --> 00:46:08.790
the corporeal activities
they enact via their mediums,

00:46:08.790 --> 00:46:11.670
and the rowdy and transgressive behaviors

00:46:11.670 --> 00:46:16.500
of oystering devotees on the
periphery of nat festivals.

00:46:16.500 --> 00:46:20.040
But unlike the transcendent Buddha,

00:46:20.040 --> 00:46:23.820
nats are the guys to whom you
bring your mundane concerns,

00:46:23.820 --> 00:46:27.660
your love affairs, your business,
your gambling aspirations.

00:46:27.660 --> 00:46:30.600
You don't, you just don't
bother the Buddha with that.

00:46:30.600 --> 00:46:34.290
It's totally improper, but
the nats are there for you.

00:46:34.290 --> 00:46:37.383
So, they get taken care of.

00:46:38.850 --> 00:46:42.630
Nat images are, of course, cheaper.

00:46:42.630 --> 00:46:44.280
The wood is of less quality.

00:46:44.280 --> 00:46:46.410
They can even be made out of plastic.

00:46:46.410 --> 00:46:48.420
Although when I was in Myanmar,

00:46:48.420 --> 00:46:50.130
it would be virtually impossible

00:46:50.130 --> 00:46:53.343
to find a Buddha image
made out of plastic.

00:46:54.480 --> 00:46:56.430
This is not an argument

00:46:56.430 --> 00:46:58.800
that the images are necessarily shabby.

00:46:58.800 --> 00:47:01.830
They're as nice as the medium can afford.

00:47:01.830 --> 00:47:05.010
And it's not an argument
that like some traditions,

00:47:05.010 --> 00:47:06.360
they're intended to decay.

00:47:06.360 --> 00:47:09.840
No, they're supposed to last
as long as they possibly can.

00:47:09.840 --> 00:47:14.840
Rather, the accessibility and
portability of these images

00:47:15.270 --> 00:47:20.163
is a reflection on the accessibility
of the nats themselves.

00:47:23.310 --> 00:47:27.074
Acquisition is a personal
rather than a community project

00:47:27.074 --> 00:47:30.210
the way commissioning a
Buddha image would be,

00:47:30.210 --> 00:47:32.130
and these small and relatively

00:47:32.130 --> 00:47:35.490
inexpensive images circulate freely.

00:47:35.490 --> 00:47:39.030
Little nat are carefully
tended by their medium

00:47:39.030 --> 00:47:41.970
and coiffed in at least some cases

00:47:41.970 --> 00:47:44.133
with the medium's own hair.

00:47:45.210 --> 00:47:48.990
Now, I was ready to go back to Korea

00:47:48.990 --> 00:47:53.070
and learn more about the images of gods

00:47:53.070 --> 00:47:57.030
that had been fighting
and fell to the floor.

00:47:57.030 --> 00:47:59.790
Of course, once we turn
to the mansin shrine,

00:47:59.790 --> 00:48:02.520
we're talking not about statue bodies,

00:48:02.520 --> 00:48:06.750
but about two-dimensional
paintings on laminated paper,

00:48:06.750 --> 00:48:08.910
and sometimes even cheap, garish,

00:48:08.910 --> 00:48:11.493
mechanically reproduced prints.

00:48:12.570 --> 00:48:17.010
Now, I knew that when
mansin install Buddha images

00:48:17.010 --> 00:48:20.910
on their altars, this is a
relatively recent practice,

00:48:20.910 --> 00:48:23.610
they mimic Buddhist eye-opening rituals

00:48:23.610 --> 00:48:26.726
or higher monks to do this for them.

00:48:26.726 --> 00:48:31.530
And in the Buddhist world of animation,

00:48:31.530 --> 00:48:36.530
the container of a statue body
has a liturgical certainty.

00:48:38.310 --> 00:48:42.540
You do the ritual properly
and it should happen.

00:48:42.540 --> 00:48:44.580
It should be enliven

00:48:44.580 --> 00:48:48.450
so long as the procedures
are carried out correctly.

00:48:48.450 --> 00:48:52.290
But when I asked how the
gods enter the paintings,

00:48:52.290 --> 00:48:56.737
I encountered a certain vagueness.

00:48:56.737 --> 00:49:00.690
"We caused the gods to be
there," one mansin said.

00:49:00.690 --> 00:49:03.810
And no, there's no
special ritual for this.

00:49:03.810 --> 00:49:06.090
The gods go into the paintings.

00:49:06.090 --> 00:49:07.890
The mansin needs them to be there

00:49:07.890 --> 00:49:10.740
in order to receive inspiration

00:49:10.740 --> 00:49:12.990
in the form of bright energy,

00:49:12.990 --> 00:49:14.730
what they call 'myonggi.'

00:49:14.730 --> 00:49:18.540
And this is supposed to happen
when the mansin sees them

00:49:18.540 --> 00:49:23.540
in front of her eyes as part
of the initiation ritual.

00:49:23.640 --> 00:49:27.570
I realized that, back in 1989,

00:49:27.570 --> 00:49:30.780
Diana Lee and I had actually captured

00:49:30.780 --> 00:49:35.133
such a moment in our film, and
I will share this with you.

00:49:36.544 --> 00:49:40.627
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:50:24.815 --> 00:50:27.065
(drumming)

00:51:32.365 --> 00:51:36.448
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:52:16.200 --> 00:52:17.977
- [Dr. Kendall] Okay, she says,

00:52:17.977 --> 00:52:21.510
"You know, if you'd gone up
there and nothing had happened,

00:52:21.510 --> 00:52:24.327
that really would've been a disaster."

00:52:26.700 --> 00:52:30.720
And so, what's happened here?

00:52:30.720 --> 00:52:33.540
She has told her, "You
have to act like a god

00:52:33.540 --> 00:52:36.390
to feel the inspiration to be the god."

00:52:36.390 --> 00:52:40.837
And then it comes through and she's told,

00:52:40.837 --> 00:52:43.530
"Go in, go into your shrine."

00:52:43.530 --> 00:52:46.320
And you know, now, you know, bow,

00:52:46.320 --> 00:52:48.960
and that connection is established.

00:52:48.960 --> 00:52:52.563
The gods have taken their
seats in the paintings.

00:52:54.570 --> 00:52:58.080
Oops. Like much in shaman practice though,

00:52:58.080 --> 00:53:00.900
it is all a bit ambiguous.

00:53:00.900 --> 00:53:03.090
It's not like you do the
ritual, the god's there.

00:53:03.090 --> 00:53:05.220
The statue enclose it.

00:53:05.220 --> 00:53:06.360
In fact,

00:53:06.360 --> 00:53:09.180
the painting is conceptualized
something like a seat,

00:53:09.180 --> 00:53:12.150
and a god can get up from that seat.

00:53:12.150 --> 00:53:14.340
The gods might not arrive at all,

00:53:14.340 --> 00:53:16.080
and the initiation might fail

00:53:16.080 --> 00:53:18.780
and the paintings go back to the shop.

00:53:18.780 --> 00:53:21.120
Or a charlatan mansin performs

00:53:21.120 --> 00:53:24.390
as though the gods were
present in the paintings,

00:53:24.390 --> 00:53:25.800
but the paintings are empty

00:53:25.800 --> 00:53:28.710
and only the other mansin know this.

00:53:28.710 --> 00:53:32.550
Or the gods might arrive,
but they don't have the force

00:53:32.550 --> 00:53:36.390
to really make the shaman
an effective shaman.

00:53:36.390 --> 00:53:39.540
As alas, that was the case
in the segment you just saw,

00:53:39.540 --> 00:53:40.620
that poor young woman,

00:53:40.620 --> 00:53:43.890
she just didn't have enough inspiration

00:53:43.890 --> 00:53:46.080
to get up the next day

00:53:46.080 --> 00:53:49.014
and be a shaman speaking
on behalf of clients,

00:53:49.014 --> 00:53:53.823
and she had to work at it further.

00:53:55.590 --> 00:53:58.320
This is an ongoing relationship too.

00:53:58.320 --> 00:54:01.650
The gods are in the
paintings, but it's ongoing.

00:54:01.650 --> 00:54:03.690
It's like a marriage. You know?

00:54:03.690 --> 00:54:06.783
They might have a falling out.

00:54:08.100 --> 00:54:09.930
The transmission of inspiration

00:54:09.930 --> 00:54:12.390
through the paintings might turn staticky

00:54:12.390 --> 00:54:14.760
because the shaman has done something

00:54:14.760 --> 00:54:18.150
that displeases the gods

00:54:18.150 --> 00:54:21.210
as when Yongsu's mother
offended her own gods

00:54:21.210 --> 00:54:23.340
by bringing in her sister's gods.

00:54:23.340 --> 00:54:24.870
And she said, "And I pray to them

00:54:24.870 --> 00:54:27.990
and I used to get this
clear flow of inspiration,

00:54:27.990 --> 00:54:31.107
but when that was going
on, it was just static."

00:54:32.730 --> 00:54:36.090
Or once present gods might
depart in a fit of peak,

00:54:36.090 --> 00:54:39.660
just leave utterly, leaving
the mansin to silence.

00:54:39.660 --> 00:54:42.210
No voice, no dreams, no visions,

00:54:42.210 --> 00:54:45.510
no body cues, or other intuition.

00:54:45.510 --> 00:54:48.630
Sometimes, the mansin
can make it right again

00:54:48.630 --> 00:54:52.803
as Yongsu's mother did, and sometimes not.

00:54:55.301 --> 00:54:59.220
Some mansin and the very
few traditionalist painters

00:54:59.220 --> 00:55:03.450
who remain in South Korea
consider the careful production

00:55:03.450 --> 00:55:04.560
of a painting.

00:55:04.560 --> 00:55:07.950
The painter engaging
with the shaman's vision

00:55:07.950 --> 00:55:12.270
and observing workshop
protocols that would be legible

00:55:12.270 --> 00:55:15.450
in the carving workshops
in Vietnam and Myanmar

00:55:15.450 --> 00:55:20.450
and Bali as essential to
the flow of inspiration

00:55:20.490 --> 00:55:22.260
through the painting.

00:55:22.260 --> 00:55:24.870
It is said that the paintings by Mr. An,

00:55:24.870 --> 00:55:27.030
whom you see in this picture,

00:55:27.030 --> 00:55:29.100
have a very high success rate

00:55:29.100 --> 00:55:32.040
when they are used in initiation rituals.

00:55:32.040 --> 00:55:35.700
And this man has more
work than he can handle.

00:55:35.700 --> 00:55:39.150
I interviewed Mr. An and
another traditionalist painter,

00:55:39.150 --> 00:55:42.330
both of whom claimed that
they sat with a mansin

00:55:42.330 --> 00:55:44.606
until they could share her vision.

00:55:44.606 --> 00:55:46.860
You know, what did you see?

00:55:46.860 --> 00:55:50.970
What color was the robe?
What gesture was the hand?

00:55:50.970 --> 00:55:53.490
And when they both feel confident

00:55:53.490 --> 00:55:57.153
that the painter can reproduce it,

00:55:58.200 --> 00:56:01.590
and then they bathe, they
report to the gods and Buddhas

00:56:01.590 --> 00:56:03.384
before beginning their work.

00:56:03.384 --> 00:56:07.050
They live like monks for the
duration of the commission

00:56:07.050 --> 00:56:09.213
in isolation and austerity.

00:56:10.350 --> 00:56:12.870
Mr. An said, "I just don't go outside.

00:56:12.870 --> 00:56:15.510
I might see an accident.
I might see a quarrel.

00:56:15.510 --> 00:56:18.570
And that would affect the painting,

00:56:18.570 --> 00:56:21.510
the whole spiritual
connection to the project."

00:56:21.510 --> 00:56:25.340
And both painters related wondrous tales

00:56:25.340 --> 00:56:29.430
of their engagements
with gods and paintings.

00:56:29.430 --> 00:56:31.260
Mansins who could afford their work,

00:56:31.260 --> 00:56:33.870
considered these paintings
a mark of distinction

00:56:33.870 --> 00:56:36.090
and good practice.

00:56:36.090 --> 00:56:39.780
But in truth, in South Korea,

00:56:39.780 --> 00:56:43.710
most mansin used paintings
that are either produced

00:56:43.710 --> 00:56:48.180
in commercial workshops
or the commercial prints.

00:56:48.180 --> 00:56:49.680
When pressed on this point,

00:56:49.680 --> 00:56:52.470
even mansin who are proud of their own

00:56:52.470 --> 00:56:55.590
traditionally produced,
very expensive paintings,

00:56:55.590 --> 00:57:00.120
will give primacy to the
mansin's connections to the gods.

00:57:00.120 --> 00:57:02.850
A mansin with strong hobby,

00:57:02.850 --> 00:57:05.100
strong compatibility with the gods,

00:57:05.100 --> 00:57:06.570
draws them to the paintings

00:57:06.570 --> 00:57:08.910
and makes them want to stay there.

00:57:08.910 --> 00:57:13.050
And even, some mansin, she said,

00:57:13.050 --> 00:57:16.500
could work even with a photograph.

00:57:16.500 --> 00:57:20.040
The shaman fosters a good
relationship with her gods

00:57:20.040 --> 00:57:21.960
through her own daily devotions.

00:57:21.960 --> 00:57:24.450
This is her responsibility,

00:57:24.450 --> 00:57:27.240
and she makes pilgrimages
to sacred mountains

00:57:27.240 --> 00:57:29.430
to recharge her spiritual batteries.

00:57:29.430 --> 00:57:34.320
This is an analogy that
they use themselves.

00:57:34.320 --> 00:57:37.293
There was a lot of electricity
talk in these interviews.

00:57:38.820 --> 00:57:40.800
I came to see the mansin's body,

00:57:40.800 --> 00:57:42.150
the shrine, and the mountain,

00:57:42.150 --> 00:57:44.670
as something like electrical circuitry.

00:57:44.670 --> 00:57:47.070
The mansin transmitting
back from the mountain

00:57:47.070 --> 00:57:50.640
to the shrine to the
gods seated in the images

00:57:50.640 --> 00:57:53.520
who provide a steady flow of inspiration

00:57:53.520 --> 00:57:58.520
in the form of words, bodily
sensations, dreams, visions,

00:57:59.340 --> 00:58:03.543
or probably most often,
just very sharp intuition.

00:58:05.370 --> 00:58:07.680
Not a fixed container like the statue,

00:58:07.680 --> 00:58:11.670
the painted image is also
more fragile, mutable.

00:58:11.670 --> 00:58:14.970
They become soiled with
incense and candle smoke

00:58:14.970 --> 00:58:18.003
and are sometimes gwaned
on by bugs or rats.

00:58:19.050 --> 00:58:23.370
Gods in the mansin world
prefer pure, clean things.

00:58:23.370 --> 00:58:25.860
When the shaman can
afford to replace them,

00:58:25.860 --> 00:58:27.397
the gods are politely told,

00:58:27.397 --> 00:58:29.820
"Can you abandon your seat for a minute?"

00:58:29.820 --> 00:58:33.000
And they take the painting down, burn it,

00:58:33.000 --> 00:58:35.370
and replace it with a nice clean one,

00:58:35.370 --> 00:58:37.473
and invite the gods back.

00:58:39.060 --> 00:58:41.550
While statues are tenderly repaired,

00:58:41.550 --> 00:58:45.780
the painting is in effect
treated like an old skin.

00:58:45.780 --> 00:58:48.630
Paintings from the shrine
of a retiring shaman,

00:58:48.630 --> 00:58:51.420
if not passed to a successor,

00:58:51.420 --> 00:58:54.060
would be buried with the
rest of her paraphernalia

00:58:54.060 --> 00:58:55.770
in a mountain grave.

00:58:55.770 --> 00:58:59.010
We do this for people and
we do this for them too.

00:58:59.010 --> 00:59:00.090
She meant the gods.

00:59:00.090 --> 00:59:03.967
A mansin explained that
the proper disposition

00:59:03.967 --> 00:59:07.830
of departed gods enacted
through the medium

00:59:07.830 --> 00:59:10.980
of paintings rotting,
corpse-like, in the ground,

00:59:10.980 --> 00:59:13.260
although of course the paintings had,

00:59:13.260 --> 00:59:15.450
the gods had been asked
to leave the paintings

00:59:15.450 --> 00:59:17.370
before they were buried.

00:59:17.370 --> 00:59:21.660
This lack of fixity, this
absence of liturgical certainty,

00:59:21.660 --> 00:59:25.800
this ultimate reliance
on the variable powers

00:59:25.800 --> 00:59:28.440
of the mansin to draw
the god's inspiration

00:59:28.440 --> 00:59:31.890
through the painting
suggests to me a distinction

00:59:31.890 --> 00:59:34.380
between the Korean mansion as a shaman,

00:59:34.380 --> 00:59:38.523
herself, a master of the
spirits fully agentive,

00:59:39.690 --> 00:59:41.933
in contrast with the (speaking
in a foreign language)

00:59:41.933 --> 00:59:42.766
In Vietnam.

00:59:42.766 --> 00:59:44.340
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:59:44.340 --> 00:59:45.690
Myanmar.

00:59:45.690 --> 00:59:48.420
Also, who, for the most part,

00:59:48.420 --> 00:59:53.250
experienced trance as more
passive vehicles of the divine,

00:59:53.250 --> 00:59:56.103
the equivalent of the statue container.

00:59:56.940 --> 01:00:00.240
In other words, by
looking outside of Korea,

01:00:00.240 --> 01:00:03.390
and then coming back to Korea again,

01:00:03.390 --> 01:00:08.070
I saw a productive contrast
that affirmed my sense

01:00:08.070 --> 01:00:10.953
of what Korean shamans are.

01:00:13.110 --> 01:00:16.590
The success of kutt is very
much a matter of the connection

01:00:16.590 --> 01:00:19.080
between the mansin and her gods.

01:00:19.080 --> 01:00:21.390
If she enjoys her favor

01:00:21.390 --> 01:00:24.090
and if her gods themselves are powerful,

01:00:24.090 --> 01:00:26.160
then they send her good inspiration.

01:00:26.160 --> 01:00:28.020
Her manifestations are compelling.

01:00:28.020 --> 01:00:32.610
Clients laugh and weep in the
presence of gods and ancestors

01:00:32.610 --> 01:00:35.580
and receive uncannily accurate

01:00:35.580 --> 01:00:40.440
or at least experientially
resonant divinations.

01:00:40.440 --> 01:00:43.560
The favor that the mansin
receives is transmitted

01:00:43.560 --> 01:00:46.170
to the favor that the client receives,

01:00:46.170 --> 01:00:48.510
and the kut is a success.

01:00:48.510 --> 01:00:51.330
The shaman's own identity and reputation

01:00:51.330 --> 01:00:55.230
are closely identified
with a success or failure

01:00:55.230 --> 01:00:57.450
of the kut she performs.

01:00:57.450 --> 01:01:00.300
Now, as an elaboration of this point,

01:01:00.300 --> 01:01:04.290
here is a brief contrast
with the expectations

01:01:04.290 --> 01:01:09.120
of a divine manifestation
by mansin in a Korean kut,

01:01:09.120 --> 01:01:12.780
and the spirit entities
who inhabit masks worn

01:01:12.780 --> 01:01:16.503
by spirit mediums in
temple festivals in Bali.

01:01:17.580 --> 01:01:21.360
In Bali, local tutelary
gods, the sesuhunan,

01:01:21.360 --> 01:01:24.930
go to ground in temple masks
that have been carefully carved

01:01:24.930 --> 01:01:28.260
and ritually animated according
to exacting protocols.

01:01:28.260 --> 01:01:31.110
Very similar to what
I've been talking about.

01:01:31.110 --> 01:01:35.190
The masks of particular
temples gain renowned

01:01:35.190 --> 01:01:37.683
as particularly powerful sesuhunan.

01:01:39.360 --> 01:01:44.360
This is Jero Amerika who
resides in the private temple

01:01:44.970 --> 01:01:47.520
of the former rulers of Ubud.

01:01:47.520 --> 01:01:51.878
The mask is famous for propelling
Jero Amerika, a medium,

01:01:51.878 --> 01:01:55.620
well, through the medium,
the mask on the medium,

01:01:55.620 --> 01:01:58.530
on long itinerating journeys around

01:01:58.530 --> 01:02:00.733
and beyond the town of Ubud

01:02:00.733 --> 01:02:04.560
to neutralize practitioners of black magic

01:02:04.560 --> 01:02:06.063
to protect the town.

01:02:07.020 --> 01:02:10.440
I saw Jero Amerika in action once

01:02:10.440 --> 01:02:14.220
and heard many accounts
of other occasions.

01:02:14.220 --> 01:02:16.890
The medium was in the innermost courtyard

01:02:16.890 --> 01:02:19.680
on that one occasion when I was present.

01:02:19.680 --> 01:02:21.120
The medium was meditating,

01:02:21.120 --> 01:02:24.570
and it was told to me by
people who was there that,

01:02:24.570 --> 01:02:28.800
ahead of schedule, the medium
is inspired, puts on the mask,

01:02:28.800 --> 01:02:32.850
comes down to the temple,
courtyard, does a few turns,

01:02:32.850 --> 01:02:34.710
and then runs out of the temple,

01:02:34.710 --> 01:02:37.410
followed by all these guys

01:02:37.410 --> 01:02:39.630
who've been waiting for this moment.

01:02:39.630 --> 01:02:42.240
And they go for the next several hours,

01:02:42.240 --> 01:02:44.640
all through the streets of Ubud,

01:02:44.640 --> 01:02:48.900
and it all get, and people
fall into very violent trance.

01:02:48.900 --> 01:02:51.480
It all gets posted on social media.

01:02:51.480 --> 01:02:56.480
And you know, as was related
to me by the people in my hotel

01:02:56.808 --> 01:02:59.373
who knew where I had
been the night before.

01:03:01.830 --> 01:03:06.120
So, I wanted to, of course, I
wanted to interview the medium

01:03:06.120 --> 01:03:09.060
and using a field assistance connections

01:03:09.060 --> 01:03:13.110
with the interior of the Palace of Ubud,

01:03:13.110 --> 01:03:17.190
we were given a name of the spirit medium

01:03:17.190 --> 01:03:20.880
who we were told had done the ritual,

01:03:20.880 --> 01:03:24.210
and we spent the better part
of a day tracking him down.

01:03:24.210 --> 01:03:28.050
We chased and chased. He
was here. No, he was there.

01:03:28.050 --> 01:03:30.393
And then finally, we got a hold of him,

01:03:31.470 --> 01:03:33.750
and he said, "No, that wasn't me.

01:03:33.750 --> 01:03:35.730
They thought it was me
'cause I'm a famous guy,

01:03:35.730 --> 01:03:36.810
but that wasn't me.

01:03:36.810 --> 01:03:38.430
That was somebody else."

01:03:38.430 --> 01:03:41.643
Now, that would never happen
with a mansin and a kut.

01:03:43.320 --> 01:03:47.130
It's her could and the
gods working through her.

01:03:47.130 --> 01:03:50.700
In Bali, it's the sesuhunan

01:03:50.700 --> 01:03:52.650
who've gone to ground in the mask,

01:03:52.650 --> 01:03:55.583
and it's the mask much
more than the medium.

01:03:55.583 --> 01:03:59.550
Albeit the medium is not inconsequential,

01:03:59.550 --> 01:04:02.703
but that really makes it happen.

01:04:05.040 --> 01:04:08.430
Okay, so in conclusion,

01:04:08.430 --> 01:04:12.390
the aim of this journey has
not been to make a typology,

01:04:12.390 --> 01:04:16.590
or god forbid, a particularly
Asian interpretation.

01:04:16.590 --> 01:04:20.820
Rather, it is to present a
wide ranging conversation

01:04:20.820 --> 01:04:23.940
enabled by some broad general principles

01:04:23.940 --> 01:04:28.470
about materiality and magic
among mediums and shamans,

01:04:28.470 --> 01:04:32.310
carvers and painters in my four examples.

01:04:32.310 --> 01:04:33.690
As I moved through them,

01:04:33.690 --> 01:04:37.620
things learned in one place
could be productively upended

01:04:37.620 --> 01:04:40.950
in another domain of
popular religious practice.

01:04:40.950 --> 01:04:44.520
Statue workshops in
Vietnam introduced the idea

01:04:44.520 --> 01:04:48.120
of statue making as an unfolding
of craftsmanship, protocol,

01:04:48.120 --> 01:04:53.010
and magic to produce an
efficacious agentive object.

01:04:53.010 --> 01:04:56.250
The Myanmar example offered
a variation on the theme.

01:04:56.250 --> 01:04:59.250
Nat images are made more casually

01:04:59.250 --> 01:05:00.780
and at less cost.

01:05:00.780 --> 01:05:04.110
Material conditions that
reflect the nats' subordination

01:05:04.110 --> 01:05:05.280
to the Buddha

01:05:05.280 --> 01:05:09.300
and the general accessibility
and portability of nat.

01:05:09.300 --> 01:05:13.687
Balinese carvers would see
something of themselves

01:05:13.687 --> 01:05:17.640
in the Burmese and
Vietnamese counterparts.

01:05:17.640 --> 01:05:20.880
Paper images in Korean shaman shrines

01:05:20.880 --> 01:05:23.940
counter the gravitas
of the statue container

01:05:23.940 --> 01:05:27.630
with a more ambiguous spirit presence

01:05:27.630 --> 01:05:29.970
that witnesses the god's more fluid

01:05:29.970 --> 01:05:33.090
and dynamic relationship with the mansin.

01:05:33.090 --> 01:05:35.730
The importance of the mansin's agency

01:05:35.730 --> 01:05:38.790
in sustaining the flow of inspiration.

01:05:38.790 --> 01:05:43.650
Something I would not have
appreciated to the same degree

01:05:43.650 --> 01:05:46.800
had I not used what I had learned in Korea

01:05:46.800 --> 01:05:49.740
to make questions posed in other places

01:05:49.740 --> 01:05:51.630
that generated questions

01:05:51.630 --> 01:05:55.680
and some surprising answers back in Korea.

01:05:55.680 --> 01:05:59.280
Less a comparison than a dialogue.

01:05:59.280 --> 01:06:02.970
And speaking of dialogue, I
want to end this presentation

01:06:02.970 --> 01:06:06.690
with an expression of gratitude
to my research partners

01:06:06.690 --> 01:06:08.430
and to all of you for staying with me

01:06:08.430 --> 01:06:10.290
through this rather long presentation.

01:06:10.290 --> 01:06:11.123
Thank you.

01:06:16.020 --> 01:06:17.040
- [Audience Member] Thank you so much.

01:06:17.040 --> 01:06:19.833
This was fascinating, to say the least.

01:06:22.320 --> 01:06:25.610
Please excuse my ignorance
in advance, but...

01:06:28.110 --> 01:06:30.663
Once a statue is unsouled,

01:06:33.002 --> 01:06:37.260
does the spirit stay
in it or does it leave?

01:06:37.260 --> 01:06:42.260
And then there's a need for
another ritual to call it back?

01:06:44.100 --> 01:06:46.770
And does it depend on where the statue is?

01:06:46.770 --> 01:06:50.160
Let's say, it's in the temple,

01:06:50.160 --> 01:06:52.653
therefore the spirit stays.

01:06:54.900 --> 01:06:56.770
Could you enlighten us on this?

01:06:56.770 --> 01:07:01.440
- Okay. As I understand
it, all the activity

01:07:01.440 --> 01:07:06.440
around the statue, around
the mask, around the,

01:07:07.951 --> 01:07:12.510
well, in the three places
that use carved wooden objects

01:07:12.510 --> 01:07:17.040
is on the assumption that
the deity should stay.

01:07:17.040 --> 01:07:20.970
Now, there are occasions where, again,

01:07:20.970 --> 01:07:22.230
the deity sent away.

01:07:22.230 --> 01:07:24.930
You need to repair your statue.

01:07:24.930 --> 01:07:29.130
And there's special protocols for that.

01:07:29.130 --> 01:07:30.870
There's also, some disagreement, you know.

01:07:30.870 --> 01:07:34.860
Do you have to do a full desacralization

01:07:34.860 --> 01:07:38.580
if you are just going to
repair it inside the temple

01:07:38.580 --> 01:07:41.100
versus taking it to the carver's workshop?

01:07:41.100 --> 01:07:43.080
You know, these little nitty gritty things

01:07:43.080 --> 01:07:46.260
that people do argue about.

01:07:46.260 --> 01:07:49.677
I heard stories in particular,

01:07:49.677 --> 01:07:54.060
one of the encounters that got
me interested in all of this.

01:07:54.060 --> 01:07:57.300
A village where during an
anti-superstition campaign,

01:07:57.300 --> 01:07:59.610
they were ordered to take the statues down

01:07:59.610 --> 01:08:02.010
and throw them in the village pond.

01:08:02.010 --> 01:08:04.237
And women describe praying, you know,

01:08:04.237 --> 01:08:06.450
"Please leave and please forgive us.

01:08:06.450 --> 01:08:09.240
It's the government that's
doing this. It's not us.

01:08:09.240 --> 01:08:10.710
It's their fault."

01:08:10.710 --> 01:08:15.180
And you know, to enable
the gods to depart.

01:08:15.180 --> 01:08:18.750
There are stories throughout
the Hindu Buddhist world,

01:08:18.750 --> 01:08:21.960
there's some understanding
that, as in the Catholic,

01:08:21.960 --> 01:08:25.980
a Catholic statue loses its
blessing if it's banged up.

01:08:25.980 --> 01:08:28.290
And that's, you know,
that's not an incarnation,

01:08:28.290 --> 01:08:31.740
that's just a, you know,
a special grace state

01:08:31.740 --> 01:08:33.690
in this world where the gods are there.

01:08:33.690 --> 01:08:35.310
There is also some sense that,

01:08:35.310 --> 01:08:39.150
if it's banged up and
disrespected, the gods leave.

01:08:39.150 --> 01:08:41.880
But that's, you know, that deals,

01:08:41.880 --> 01:08:45.690
that has to do with the inadequacy

01:08:45.690 --> 01:08:50.690
of the material container,
or I guess, you know,

01:08:51.990 --> 01:08:56.370
true acts of desecration,
like iconoclastic moments.

01:08:56.370 --> 01:08:58.740
So, that's an interesting comment.

01:08:58.740 --> 01:09:02.460
Whereas in Korea, you know,
they go up, they go down,

01:09:02.460 --> 01:09:05.550
it's a much less permanent thing.

01:09:05.550 --> 01:09:07.680
Because it's a relationship

01:09:07.680 --> 01:09:09.963
that has to constantly be reaffirmed.

01:09:10.904 --> 01:09:12.053
- [Audience Member] Hi,
thank you so much for coming.

01:09:12.053 --> 01:09:14.370
This is so interesting.

01:09:14.370 --> 01:09:15.750
I was wondering if you had like,

01:09:15.750 --> 01:09:18.180
maybe like a personal experience
with Korean shamanism,

01:09:18.180 --> 01:09:20.641
because I'm assuming you were
in a close proximity to it.

01:09:20.641 --> 01:09:23.160
And I wonder if you had the opportunity

01:09:23.160 --> 01:09:26.310
to experience maybe
contacting your own ancestors.

01:09:26.310 --> 01:09:27.180
Just outta curiosity.

01:09:27.180 --> 01:09:28.260
- Contacting your own ancestors?

01:09:28.260 --> 01:09:30.333
Well, through the shamans, yes.

01:09:31.554 --> 01:09:36.554
I have sponsored quote for the
dead on behalf of my parents,

01:09:38.280 --> 01:09:43.280
and I've been addressed by my dead parents

01:09:43.440 --> 01:09:46.110
who speak much better Korean than I do.

01:09:46.110 --> 01:09:49.200
And, but you know, with some americanisms

01:09:49.200 --> 01:09:50.943
like "Okay" thrown in.

01:09:52.560 --> 01:09:57.470
I had one of those twilight
experiences where...

01:10:00.360 --> 01:10:02.340
When my father was ill with cancer,

01:10:02.340 --> 01:10:04.327
I remember him touching
my back and saying,

01:10:04.327 --> 01:10:05.610
"Oh, I can feel your ribs,"

01:10:05.610 --> 01:10:07.980
and me thinking you shouldn't
be worried about my weight.

01:10:07.980 --> 01:10:10.710
We're all worried about your weight.

01:10:10.710 --> 01:10:15.457
And then when we were doing our,

01:10:16.530 --> 01:10:19.500
I sponsored a ritual after his death

01:10:19.500 --> 01:10:23.460
and the shaman comes up, incarnating,

01:10:23.460 --> 01:10:26.095
manifesting my father and
feels my back and says,

01:10:26.095 --> 01:10:27.330
(speaking in a foreign language)

01:10:27.330 --> 01:10:29.160
You know, I can feel your bones.

01:10:29.160 --> 01:10:31.050
I can feel your ribs.

01:10:31.050 --> 01:10:33.750
And I thought, "Whoo, this is weird."

01:10:33.750 --> 01:10:35.900
You know, how did she,
you know, this is...

01:10:37.380 --> 01:10:39.570
And I thought it's all in my head.

01:10:39.570 --> 01:10:42.783
I imagined it, but no,
Diana Lee got it on film.

01:10:44.670 --> 01:10:47.790
I can't say all of my
encounters are that vivid,

01:10:47.790 --> 01:10:51.660
but that one, that one stands out for me.

01:10:51.660 --> 01:10:53.820
- [Audience Member] Thank
you for the fascinating talk.

01:10:53.820 --> 01:10:56.850
I found myself having
lots of different thoughts

01:10:56.850 --> 01:10:58.710
about different things
I've seen over the years

01:10:58.710 --> 01:11:01.320
that I've realize now I
probably didn't understand,

01:11:01.320 --> 01:11:02.970
and my question may well reveal

01:11:02.970 --> 01:11:05.195
just how little I understand.

01:11:05.195 --> 01:11:10.195
But I was thinking about stupas
and relics and the way...

01:11:13.440 --> 01:11:17.250
Even, so, in the Beinecke library here,

01:11:17.250 --> 01:11:19.860
we have the Hyakumanto Darani.

01:11:19.860 --> 01:11:23.670
And my understanding of the
efficaciousness of the darani

01:11:23.670 --> 01:11:25.470
was that there were
supposed to be the words

01:11:25.470 --> 01:11:27.360
spoken by the Buddha.

01:11:27.360 --> 01:11:29.910
And so, I was thinking
about how all of that works

01:11:29.910 --> 01:11:31.350
and whether there are connections there.

01:11:31.350 --> 01:11:32.700
And then it also led me to thinking

01:11:32.700 --> 01:11:34.830
about the question of mummies.

01:11:34.830 --> 01:11:37.150
So, instead of what we would consider

01:11:38.340 --> 01:11:40.983
an inanimate object being animated,

01:11:42.090 --> 01:11:44.460
you have the kind of opposite where...

01:11:44.460 --> 01:11:47.850
what we would've once considered
to be an animate object

01:11:47.850 --> 01:11:49.320
seems no longer to be,

01:11:49.320 --> 01:11:54.303
and yet continues to be
revered as if still alive.

01:11:55.560 --> 01:11:57.600
Sorry, I'm revealing
my own prejudices here

01:11:57.600 --> 01:11:59.340
with the way I'm saying
that, but you know.

01:11:59.340 --> 01:12:02.910
And I'm just wondering if
you see connections there,

01:12:02.910 --> 01:12:07.910
generally across north and
southeast Asia as well.

01:12:08.070 --> 01:12:13.070
- Okay. Mummies, I will say
I know pretty much zero.

01:12:13.410 --> 01:12:15.720
Are you talking about those
traditions where like a Daoist

01:12:15.720 --> 01:12:18.540
is supposed to eat a certain kind of wood,

01:12:18.540 --> 01:12:23.010
and then their body is preserved
from putrefaction or...

01:12:23.010 --> 01:12:26.310
- I was thinking mainly of Koya-San

01:12:26.310 --> 01:12:30.093
and (indistinct) who's
supposedly still alive.

01:12:31.800 --> 01:12:35.370
And everyday, the monks bring him his food

01:12:35.370 --> 01:12:40.260
and he's supposedly
sitting there meditating

01:12:40.260 --> 01:12:41.673
hundreds of years later.

01:12:43.381 --> 01:12:47.670
So, sorry, I'm not very coherent.

01:12:47.670 --> 01:12:49.891
This is not really a
question, but I kind of-

01:12:49.891 --> 01:12:50.885
- No, it's interesting.

01:12:50.885 --> 01:12:53.180
I mean, these are all topics for you

01:12:53.180 --> 01:12:55.080
or your students to take on.

01:12:55.080 --> 01:12:57.500
These are, you know...

01:12:58.650 --> 01:13:02.640
This is all under the
banner of material religion.

01:13:02.640 --> 01:13:06.450
There, you know, as, you
may know, some people know,

01:13:06.450 --> 01:13:09.720
my students get very upset
when people use the word object

01:13:09.720 --> 01:13:12.330
because they've all taken
a course on thing theory.

01:13:12.330 --> 01:13:14.460
And if it's not an artifact,
you call it a thing

01:13:14.460 --> 01:13:18.390
because it's so much more than
just the material limitations

01:13:18.390 --> 01:13:19.860
of what it is.

01:13:19.860 --> 01:13:23.820
And there are so many things
that could be studied to,

01:13:23.820 --> 01:13:25.350
you know, how do people think about 'em?

01:13:25.350 --> 01:13:27.330
How do people argue about
what they're supposed

01:13:27.330 --> 01:13:28.410
to think about them?

01:13:28.410 --> 01:13:31.500
What's the proper care and
feeding of such an object?

01:13:31.500 --> 01:13:36.500
And, you know, it's, there
are infinite examples.

01:13:36.810 --> 01:13:40.110
There's all kinds of interesting
work that can be done.

01:13:40.110 --> 01:13:41.673
With respect to the mummy,

01:13:43.140 --> 01:13:46.530
I guess the thing most resonant to me is,

01:13:46.530 --> 01:13:49.380
what is it about the human form?

01:13:49.380 --> 01:13:52.230
These are all, you know,
in one instance, the mask,

01:13:52.230 --> 01:13:54.180
it's just the head with the medium body,

01:13:54.180 --> 01:13:56.190
but the others are all...

01:13:56.190 --> 01:13:59.190
What does it mean to
replicate a human form?

01:13:59.190 --> 01:14:02.073
Does it conjure a presence?

01:14:02.940 --> 01:14:03.990
Museum mannequins,

01:14:03.990 --> 01:14:07.260
people have all these Night at
the Museum responses to them

01:14:07.260 --> 01:14:10.200
because they're human
forms in human sizes,

01:14:10.200 --> 01:14:12.603
not like big statues on pedestals.

01:14:14.490 --> 01:14:17.370
It's just a big sea that, you know,

01:14:17.370 --> 01:14:20.463
to jump in and swim in and get more from.

01:14:21.540 --> 01:14:22.703
- [Audience Member] Thank you.

01:14:25.110 --> 01:14:27.630
I know nothing about the spiritual world,

01:14:27.630 --> 01:14:32.220
So, it was really eyeopening
for me to see that world

01:14:32.220 --> 01:14:35.853
through things as you did.

01:14:36.870 --> 01:14:41.190
I had this preconceived
concept that the spirit mediums

01:14:41.190 --> 01:14:45.990
had this special connection
to the spiritual world

01:14:45.990 --> 01:14:49.770
and that object as
supplements to the world.

01:14:49.770 --> 01:14:54.770
But the way you paint
it is how that no one,

01:14:54.870 --> 01:14:59.870
including the mediums, can
reach the spiritual world along,

01:14:59.940 --> 01:15:03.120
and actually they need the objects

01:15:03.120 --> 01:15:05.940
or paintings to get inspired.

01:15:05.940 --> 01:15:10.940
Therefore, it's the carvers
or painters are critical part

01:15:11.820 --> 01:15:16.820
of the efforts and how incredibly
communal that effort is,

01:15:18.150 --> 01:15:22.470
and how it is not an
individual relationship

01:15:22.470 --> 01:15:24.330
to the spirituality.

01:15:24.330 --> 01:15:29.330
And I imagine that that
communality or how that belief,

01:15:31.020 --> 01:15:34.980
the spiritual relationship
is only possible

01:15:34.980 --> 01:15:39.980
through the communal collective
efforts is heightened

01:15:40.890 --> 01:15:43.830
and highlighted through rituals.

01:15:43.830 --> 01:15:48.540
But I was wondering, in a
mundane, everyday settings,

01:15:48.540 --> 01:15:52.740
how the kind of collective relationality

01:15:52.740 --> 01:15:57.740
to spirituality manifests
perhaps differently,

01:15:57.960 --> 01:16:00.890
or how that, no...

01:16:01.740 --> 01:16:06.367
Just the practiced or felt in that moment

01:16:07.530 --> 01:16:12.180
when that's not in a no
spiritual performative moment.

01:16:12.180 --> 01:16:13.980
- Okay, that's a very good question.

01:16:13.980 --> 01:16:15.442
Thank you. I mean...

01:16:15.442 --> 01:16:19.920
These different representations inhabit

01:16:19.920 --> 01:16:23.373
different sociality social worlds.

01:16:24.643 --> 01:16:29.643
In the case of the Korean
shamans, every day, they...

01:16:30.450 --> 01:16:34.110
In the morning, they burn
candles. They offer water.

01:16:34.110 --> 01:16:38.850
This is so, and they
address their deities.

01:16:38.850 --> 01:16:42.570
And then there is this
kind of playful, ongoing,

01:16:42.570 --> 01:16:44.400
the shaman receives a gift,

01:16:44.400 --> 01:16:46.830
it goes first onto the altar,

01:16:46.830 --> 01:16:50.280
and it's essence gets
consumed by the deities,

01:16:50.280 --> 01:16:53.520
and then she takes it back.

01:16:53.520 --> 01:16:55.500
Sometimes, there'll be a dream.

01:16:55.500 --> 01:16:56.640
One of the deities,

01:16:56.640 --> 01:17:00.003
and the mansin I worked with most closely,

01:17:00.930 --> 01:17:03.900
one of the deities was...

01:17:03.900 --> 01:17:06.060
The person appointed to be that deity

01:17:06.060 --> 01:17:09.060
was her dead husband who
had been an alcoholic.

01:17:09.060 --> 01:17:11.520
And every now, she described one incident

01:17:11.520 --> 01:17:14.310
where she gets this wild
craving for Korean soju

01:17:14.310 --> 01:17:16.440
and has to run to the store

01:17:16.440 --> 01:17:19.710
and put all the bottles
under the painting.

01:17:19.710 --> 01:17:24.600
And so, no, it is ongoing. It is daily.

01:17:24.600 --> 01:17:29.280
But it's in the more intimate
sphere of one practitioner

01:17:29.280 --> 01:17:30.510
and her deities.

01:17:30.510 --> 01:17:35.130
Or in Myanmar, the daily
tending of these little images,

01:17:35.130 --> 01:17:38.640
it's almost like doll-like devotion.

01:17:38.640 --> 01:17:40.440
But I didn't want to talk about dolls

01:17:40.440 --> 01:17:44.220
because it's so freighted
with psychological stuff

01:17:44.220 --> 01:17:49.220
that I didn't want to impose
on the Burmese spirit mediums.

01:17:49.560 --> 01:17:51.630
But no.

01:17:51.630 --> 01:17:56.010
No, it is a sense of continuous presence.

01:17:56.010 --> 01:17:58.200
It's just that in the ritual moment,

01:17:58.200 --> 01:18:03.090
then the deity becomes manifest
in the medium or the shaman,

01:18:03.090 --> 01:18:04.020
and that's it.

01:18:04.020 --> 01:18:06.960
But the communities are
different when you make, say,

01:18:06.960 --> 01:18:10.500
a Buddhist image in a
community in Myanmar,

01:18:10.500 --> 01:18:13.850
it's the whole community
working on it when you...

01:18:15.060 --> 01:18:17.460
Well, you know, feeding the carvers,

01:18:17.460 --> 01:18:20.220
and maybe doing some Buddhist precepts

01:18:20.220 --> 01:18:21.540
to help everything along.

01:18:21.540 --> 01:18:24.810
When you carve a mask
in a Balinese community,

01:18:24.810 --> 01:18:27.122
the community is feeding the carver,

01:18:27.122 --> 01:18:29.610
and there are members of the community

01:18:29.610 --> 01:18:33.210
who are in the temple meditating
before the carving starts,

01:18:33.210 --> 01:18:37.443
and there's this great sense
of collective enterprise.

01:18:38.610 --> 01:18:39.870
The shaman work,

01:18:39.870 --> 01:18:42.930
and I would say the nat work

01:18:42.930 --> 01:18:45.600
is much more individualistic-oriented

01:18:45.600 --> 01:18:48.510
in terms of the
relationship on the one hand

01:18:48.510 --> 01:18:50.790
and the ministry of the clients

01:18:50.790 --> 01:18:53.403
who are bringing their
own special problems in.

01:18:54.626 --> 01:18:55.459
- [Audience Member] Thank you so much

01:18:55.459 --> 01:18:58.230
for such a fascinating talk.

01:18:58.230 --> 01:19:00.300
I guess my question is sort of remaining

01:19:00.300 --> 01:19:02.160
in the mundane world a little bit.

01:19:02.160 --> 01:19:06.240
And just thinking about what
happens in a couple of cases

01:19:06.240 --> 01:19:10.680
when these objects are
removed from the communities.

01:19:10.680 --> 01:19:14.400
And so, you described, you
know, there is a market

01:19:14.400 --> 01:19:16.197
in some sense or another
for these objects.

01:19:16.197 --> 01:19:19.473
And you know, the more you
pay, the better the efficacy.

01:19:21.120 --> 01:19:22.920
What happens, I mean,

01:19:22.920 --> 01:19:24.540
I feel like these days you can go

01:19:24.540 --> 01:19:29.070
to any kind of garden center or whatever

01:19:29.070 --> 01:19:30.900
and pick up a statue of a Buddha.

01:19:30.900 --> 01:19:33.420
And I wondered how your interlocutors feel

01:19:33.420 --> 01:19:36.750
about this use of these kinds of statues.

01:19:36.750 --> 01:19:39.150
And then on a more serious note,

01:19:39.150 --> 01:19:40.440
what are the implications?

01:19:40.440 --> 01:19:42.090
And I'd just love to hear
you talk a little bit

01:19:42.090 --> 01:19:44.910
about what happens when these objects

01:19:44.910 --> 01:19:47.310
become curated in museums,

01:19:47.310 --> 01:19:51.840
and how do you negotiate
those kinds of moves.

01:19:51.840 --> 01:19:54.814
- To the last part of your question,

01:19:54.814 --> 01:19:58.250
I could do a whole hour on that, but...

01:19:59.880 --> 01:20:02.490
And there's a range of stuff.

01:20:02.490 --> 01:20:06.480
There are, most of what ends,

01:20:06.480 --> 01:20:09.300
well, no, I can't generalize.

01:20:09.300 --> 01:20:12.840
Museums are getting more
savvy and sensitive now.

01:20:12.840 --> 01:20:14.730
There's writing, for example,

01:20:14.730 --> 01:20:19.730
on if it's a consecrated
Tibetan image, you can image it,

01:20:21.600 --> 01:20:25.374
do an x-ray and see if it has
consecration material in it?

01:20:25.374 --> 01:20:28.020
If it has the consecration material in it,

01:20:28.020 --> 01:20:30.483
is it still carrying the charge or not?

01:20:31.470 --> 01:20:35.130
Do you call in a Tibetan
monk to maybe revivify it,

01:20:35.130 --> 01:20:38.013
or remove it, or deal
appropriately with it?

01:20:39.090 --> 01:20:43.680
It's, at least, as varied as this stuff,

01:20:43.680 --> 01:20:47.100
I was talking about in
answer to the last question.

01:20:47.100 --> 01:20:49.890
Museums are doing different
things in different ways.

01:20:49.890 --> 01:20:51.930
But over the last 30 years,

01:20:51.930 --> 01:20:56.930
we have seen a huge uptick
in the sensitivity of museums

01:20:57.090 --> 01:21:01.380
to the prior lives and meanings
of the material in our care.

01:21:01.380 --> 01:21:04.260
In the American Museum of Natural History,

01:21:04.260 --> 01:21:05.640
we have, deep in our storage,

01:21:05.640 --> 01:21:08.010
something we call the smudge room.

01:21:08.010 --> 01:21:11.430
And it's a room, where among other things,

01:21:11.430 --> 01:21:13.650
Native Americans can come in,

01:21:13.650 --> 01:21:17.700
take sweet grass and purify objects,

01:21:17.700 --> 01:21:20.670
and it won't set off the smoke detector.

01:21:20.670 --> 01:21:24.420
The fire service will
only respond to heat.

01:21:24.420 --> 01:21:26.640
And it's also been used by Tibetans.

01:21:26.640 --> 01:21:28.860
It's been used by visiting Siberians.

01:21:28.860 --> 01:21:31.680
It's a special sacred space set apart.

01:21:31.680 --> 01:21:35.763
The museum made that commitment
to recognizing these needs.

01:21:37.260 --> 01:21:39.840
So, oh, no, there are lots
and lots and lots of stories

01:21:39.840 --> 01:21:42.450
that can be told about museum responses

01:21:42.450 --> 01:21:44.070
to the sacredness of objects,

01:21:44.070 --> 01:21:47.370
and I would say it's a
growing consciousness.

01:21:47.370 --> 01:21:50.070
When we did our new Northwest Coast Hall,

01:21:50.070 --> 01:21:53.880
you would walk down the
anthropology corridor,

01:21:53.880 --> 01:21:55.920
you know, the non-public space,

01:21:55.920 --> 01:21:59.700
and on the door of one of
the conservator's areas,

01:21:59.700 --> 01:22:02.130
if this room contains shamanic objects,

01:22:02.130 --> 01:22:04.350
they may be dangerous to certain persons.

01:22:04.350 --> 01:22:06.210
Please be aware.

01:22:06.210 --> 01:22:09.240
And then the empowered things were covered

01:22:09.240 --> 01:22:11.130
when they weren't being worked on,

01:22:11.130 --> 01:22:15.390
and it became a kind of laboratory culture

01:22:15.390 --> 01:22:17.880
that developed in
consultation with the people

01:22:17.880 --> 01:22:20.310
who were the descendant community.

01:22:20.310 --> 01:22:21.390
And we all love it.

01:22:21.390 --> 01:22:24.873
I mean, this is anthropology
comes home, full circle.

01:22:25.800 --> 01:22:28.080
And then, what was the first question?

01:22:28.080 --> 01:22:28.913
- [Audience Member] It was just,

01:22:28.913 --> 01:22:33.600
I just wonder how your
interlocutors felt about the,

01:22:33.600 --> 01:22:36.270
I guess, non-religious use-

01:22:36.270 --> 01:22:38.040
- Oh, non-religious use.

01:22:38.040 --> 01:22:38.873
Yeah.

01:22:38.873 --> 01:22:42.930
I think, again, you
would find a huge range

01:22:42.930 --> 01:22:44.377
from the people who say,

01:22:44.377 --> 01:22:47.430
"Well, it wasn't made to
be sacred, so who cares?"

01:22:47.430 --> 01:22:50.550
to the people who are saying,
"That's disrespectful,

01:22:50.550 --> 01:22:51.750
and that's disturbing,

01:22:51.750 --> 01:22:54.540
and that's only gonna bring bad luck."

01:22:54.540 --> 01:22:57.750
I mean, just, you know,
just a range of stuff.

01:22:57.750 --> 01:22:59.820
They, what I've looked at,

01:22:59.820 --> 01:23:01.680
and I do mention a bit in the book,

01:23:01.680 --> 01:23:04.530
is things that fall into the art market,

01:23:04.530 --> 01:23:07.710
and when, where, and how that happens.

01:23:07.710 --> 01:23:10.890
And you know, the stories
that might arise around that.

01:23:10.890 --> 01:23:12.637
And some shamans who say,

01:23:12.637 --> 01:23:15.450
"Well, they know. The
gods know that, you know,

01:23:15.450 --> 01:23:17.940
the shop isn't for them,
the museum isn't for them.

01:23:17.940 --> 01:23:19.077
They take off."

01:23:21.000 --> 01:23:24.450
And with the statue, some
general assumption again

01:23:24.450 --> 01:23:27.090
that they have been deactivated.

01:23:27.090 --> 01:23:30.307
And some dealers who will
treat things with respect,

01:23:30.307 --> 01:23:33.417
"I'd never put it on the
floor. It goes on a shelf."

01:23:34.499 --> 01:23:36.210
- [Audience Member] Can
I ask you a little more

01:23:36.210 --> 01:23:37.950
about the materials going in the niches

01:23:37.950 --> 01:23:39.180
in the back of a statutory?

01:23:39.180 --> 01:23:41.850
I know a lot of work's being
done in Chinese Buddhism

01:23:41.850 --> 01:23:43.020
for what's in,

01:23:43.020 --> 01:23:44.610
can you say a little bit
about the relationship

01:23:44.610 --> 01:23:46.410
in different countries
or different figures

01:23:46.410 --> 01:23:49.110
between what goes in and
the type of ensoulment-

01:23:49.110 --> 01:23:50.550
- Oh, okay.

01:23:50.550 --> 01:23:51.840
- [Audience Member] Or
anything on that line.

01:23:51.840 --> 01:23:53.383
- Okay. They will say, I mean,

01:23:53.383 --> 01:23:58.383
what I've heard is that
even within Vietnam,

01:23:58.740 --> 01:24:02.070
it's highly variable.

01:24:02.070 --> 01:24:05.310
I haven't heard in Vietnam,
for example, of putting,

01:24:05.310 --> 01:24:08.493
but you know, and then you
compare and it's variable.

01:24:10.290 --> 01:24:15.290
But in general, it's five
precious metals or substances.

01:24:16.620 --> 01:24:19.140
It's sometimes five colored strings.

01:24:19.140 --> 01:24:21.570
You know, the colors of the cosmos.

01:24:21.570 --> 01:24:25.470
And some people say that's the intestines,

01:24:25.470 --> 01:24:30.120
a lot of amuletic
material, sometimes metal.

01:24:30.120 --> 01:24:32.370
I have seen the interiors,

01:24:32.370 --> 01:24:34.920
the stuff come out of Tibetan statues

01:24:34.920 --> 01:24:37.140
that includes even things
like watch bearings

01:24:37.140 --> 01:24:39.600
because they were
probably the right metal,

01:24:39.600 --> 01:24:41.910
but I don't know for sure.

01:24:41.910 --> 01:24:44.190
I have heard in,

01:24:44.190 --> 01:24:46.260
well, there's been a lot of work done

01:24:46.260 --> 01:24:49.020
on this cache of statues in China

01:24:49.020 --> 01:24:51.840
that contain various material medica

01:24:51.840 --> 01:24:56.818
related to the work the statue's
supposed to do in Taiwan.

01:24:56.818 --> 01:25:00.000
Sometimes poisonous bugs and insects.

01:25:00.000 --> 01:25:02.490
And then some people say, "But
that's never for a Buddha,

01:25:02.490 --> 01:25:03.990
that's for the ones that are supposed

01:25:03.990 --> 01:25:05.670
to help you in business."

01:25:05.670 --> 01:25:07.053
Now, that's aggressive.

01:25:08.700 --> 01:25:12.367
So, no, highly variable but
recognizable where people say,

01:25:12.367 --> 01:25:13.710
"Oh, yeah, unlike me.

01:25:13.710 --> 01:25:16.500
You know, what? You
put stuff in the statue

01:25:16.500 --> 01:25:18.840
and bring it to life, and
I'd never heard of it?"

01:25:18.840 --> 01:25:20.460
This is a very, you know,

01:25:20.460 --> 01:25:22.950
comfortable acknowledgement
across the span

01:25:22.950 --> 01:25:24.423
that I've been talking about.

01:25:25.740 --> 01:25:27.810
- [Audience Member] So, I'm
interested in the combination

01:25:27.810 --> 01:25:29.490
of statues in this picture,

01:25:29.490 --> 01:25:32.940
I'm wondering, are they
just a random bundle?

01:25:32.940 --> 01:25:35.280
Or like, are they combined together

01:25:35.280 --> 01:25:37.440
to add up to the efficacy?

01:25:37.440 --> 01:25:39.000
- Are they combined to,

01:25:39.000 --> 01:25:43.800
they are the nat who have been identified

01:25:43.800 --> 01:25:48.800
as working with a particular
medium on the basis of dreams,

01:25:49.590 --> 01:25:54.590
on the basis of the nat goes to a festival

01:25:54.960 --> 01:25:57.840
and the music for a
particular nats played,

01:25:57.840 --> 01:26:00.727
and he or she dances wildly and they say,

01:26:00.727 --> 01:26:03.120
"Oh, you've gotta observe them."

01:26:03.120 --> 01:26:06.540
And then they get an image at some point.

01:26:06.540 --> 01:26:08.280
And the quest for the perfect image

01:26:08.280 --> 01:26:09.930
can be quite interesting too.

01:26:09.930 --> 01:26:13.293
There are different stories
about finding the right statue.

01:26:15.060 --> 01:26:15.893
- [Audience Member] Okay.

01:26:15.893 --> 01:26:17.790
Why don't I just ask the last question?

01:26:17.790 --> 01:26:19.710
A more broader question about shamanism,

01:26:19.710 --> 01:26:23.313
and then we can close the event.

01:26:24.750 --> 01:26:28.980
So, do you think shamanism
is on the rise now?

01:26:28.980 --> 01:26:31.260
'Cause I think-
- Think it's on the rise.

01:26:31.260 --> 01:26:35.250
Oh, gosh, it's so hard to quantify.

01:26:35.250 --> 01:26:37.140
If you had asked the reverse question,

01:26:37.140 --> 01:26:39.450
do I think it's dying
out now, it's irrelevant.

01:26:39.450 --> 01:26:40.500
I'd say absolutely not.

01:26:40.500 --> 01:26:41.470
- [Audience Member] Absolutely not, okay.

01:26:41.470 --> 01:26:44.580
- Yeah, it seems to be flourishing.

01:26:44.580 --> 01:26:48.616
But, you know, you have
to weigh that against,

01:26:48.616 --> 01:26:52.470
you know, to the degree to
which it had to be underground

01:26:52.470 --> 01:26:53.880
at certain periods.

01:26:53.880 --> 01:26:56.640
So, is it more now or is it
just more out in the open?

01:26:56.640 --> 01:26:57.663
I don't know.

01:26:58.530 --> 01:27:00.810
There are organizations in South Korea

01:27:00.810 --> 01:27:03.030
that claim they have
statistics on shamans.

01:27:03.030 --> 01:27:03.930
I don't believe it.

01:27:03.930 --> 01:27:06.450
They have statistics on people who admit,

01:27:06.450 --> 01:27:08.070
people who've paid admission

01:27:08.070 --> 01:27:11.430
to a particular shaman organization.

01:27:11.430 --> 01:27:13.980
There are many, many more
I'm sure than the statistics.

01:27:13.980 --> 01:27:16.290
But people also fall out of the practice

01:27:16.290 --> 01:27:18.090
even though they're not supposed to.

01:27:19.410 --> 01:27:21.000
So, I would just say, yes, there are,

01:27:21.000 --> 01:27:23.430
it's emphatically not on decline.

01:27:23.430 --> 01:27:24.630
- [Audience Member] Mm-hm. Mm-hm.

01:27:24.630 --> 01:27:27.300
Yeah, because I'm teaching Korean religion

01:27:27.300 --> 01:27:31.200
and culture course, often,
students say that their parents,

01:27:31.200 --> 01:27:33.720
although they're Catholic or Christians,

01:27:33.720 --> 01:27:36.030
they often consult with
fortune tellers and shamans.

01:27:36.030 --> 01:27:40.127
So, apparently, although they
had not reveal their practice,

01:27:40.127 --> 01:27:44.250
but definitely a lot of people
are practicing shamanism.

01:27:44.250 --> 01:27:45.083
And then-

01:27:45.083 --> 01:27:47.040
- No, and there is also,

01:27:47.040 --> 01:27:49.260
here's something that might account

01:27:49.260 --> 01:27:50.310
for what seems like more.

01:27:50.310 --> 01:27:53.580
There are people who,
you know, 40 years ago,

01:27:53.580 --> 01:27:57.000
they would just call themselves
a fortune teller, a diviner,

01:27:57.000 --> 01:27:59.670
and not a really initiated shaman.

01:27:59.670 --> 01:28:03.450
Nowadays, I think the
impulse is to cast yourself

01:28:03.450 --> 01:28:05.880
as a shaman to even wear robes and all,

01:28:05.880 --> 01:28:07.830
even though whether or not, you know,

01:28:07.830 --> 01:28:11.100
these old ladies that I worked
with would recognize them

01:28:11.100 --> 01:28:13.803
as shamans because
there's more cache to it.

01:28:15.479 --> 01:28:17.407
- Then thank you so much
for your illuminating talk

01:28:18.910 --> 01:28:22.077
and thank you so much for just coming.

